{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN.md | sed -n '1,190p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The product comparison that decides release (D665): run plan, version 12 (D668–D672 amendments)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Amendments in force over the text below:** the scored window is 2026-09-07 to 09-20 with the warm-up 07-01 to 09-06 unfiltered (D669); 410 kept articles (D670); N = 32 (D671); the scorer, the card builder and the §1.6 tools are built during the run against `stage0/verdict-rule.json` and `stage0/card-spec.md`, and reviewed before any output is read (D672); the slice stands in for the dry run (D672). Where §1.2 and §1.3 still say 07-20 or 08-31, D669 governs.\n     4\t\n     5\tv9 answers rounds 7 and 8 (`review-codex-7.md`, `review-codex-8.md`). The Claude reviewer found v7 READY. The decisions this plan takes are drafted in `D667-draft.md`.\n     6\t\n     7\t**Alex:**\n     8\t- D664: \"no need for budget … run your council to determine\".\n     9\t- D666: \"okay then you should write a run plan and monitor it carefully and don't scope drift\".\n    10\t\n    11\t**Read as:** his word to run this comparison once it passes its council review. Nothing outside it is started. The plan applies\n    12\t`clean-arch/PRD.md` §1.5 (D665) and does not change it.\n    13\t\n    14\t**How review works from here (`ROUND-LOG.md`, round 2):**\n    15\t- This plan states each requirement and how it is proved.\n    16\t- Prose review ends when a round finds no gap in the design.\n    17\t- Everything build-level is checked on the built artifacts at the stage-0 **freeze review**: code with its tests and mutants, the frozen\n    18\t  files and the dry runs. That review, by Codex and a fresh Claude reader, gates the first paid send.\n    19\t\n    20\t## 0. Scope\n    21\t\n    22\t**In:**\n    23\t- poc-v118 against the new system (astra-frozen), from empty at 2026-07-01, three draws each;\n    24\t- the blind matter census;\n    25\t- the council's reading;\n    26\t- D665's verdict and everything D665 prints beside it;\n    27\t- the report to Alex.\n    28\t\n    29\t**Out** (written to `FINDINGS.md`, not acted on):\n    30\t- any prompt or answer-choice change (D662);\n    31\t- model trials;\n    32\t- live-store, planner or integration work;\n    33\t- D658;\n    34\t- other weeks or companies;\n    35\t- any \"while we are here\" fix.\n    36\t\n    37\t## 1. Stage 0 ($0): built, proved, frozen, then reviewed\n    38\t\n    39\t### 1.1 Versions manifest\n    40\t\n    41\t`manifest.json` holds the sha256 of:\n    42\t- every file either system runs, including poc-v118's configuration and the frozen ladder request;\n    43\t- the filtered items copy;\n    44\t- every instrument below.\n    45\t\n    46\tThe launchers refuse a file that differs.\n    47\t\n    48\t### 1.2 Exposure census, exclusions, one input\n    49\t\n    50\t1. **The population.** The clean selection rule's articles from 07-20 to 09-20: 1,619 articles (by week: 185, 164, 62, 114, 109, 265, 218, 229,\n    51\t   273). The warm-up from 07-01 to 07-19 has 357 articles.\n    52\t2. **What counts as a read** (the closed set, frozen before any hit is read). The article's id or its text sha appears in any of:\n    53\t   - a run's trace, archive or ledger (either system);\n    54\t   - a probe;\n    55\t   - a gold or reference;\n    56\t   - a reader packet, answer or tally;\n    57\t   - a design, result or log document;\n    58\t   - a report;\n    59\t   - a test fixture, mock or UI-check data file.\n    60\t\n    61\t   **Not reads:**\n    62\t   - **documented corpus storage, by content:**\n    63\t     - `corpus-builds.json`, frozen before any hit is read, lists the documented corpus builds: the current source, `items-window.sqlite`,\n    64\t       `items-window-tags2-2026-10-24.sqlite`, the page store, and any other build the decision log or `data-manifest.md` records as a corpus.\n    65\t       Each comes with its record and its set of input ids.\n    66\t     - A table is exempt only when it is a **verified raw copy** of a listed build: the same rows, by `input_id` and `content_sha256`. That\n    67\t       covers renamed copies.\n    68\t     - Any other such table (a probe's or fixture's selection) is searched like everything else.\n    69\t     - The exemption covers stored rows only. Whatever a probe, run or fixture actually used, even from a whole-corpus copy, is a read\n    70\t       through its trace, packet or fixture text.\n    71\t     - Every other table in the same file, and every other file, is searched;\n    72\t   - the search index built over the whole corpus;\n    73\t   - this comparison's own files.\n    74\t\n    75\t   Git-ignored files are searched.\n    76\t3. **Where it is searched:**\n    77\t   - the whole of this checkout, and of every worktree `git worktree list` shows, except `.git` (`tests/`, `poc/mock` and `poc/ui`\n    78\t     included). The exempt rows are handled as above;\n    79\t   - the archived runs in `docs/research/2026-10-25-english/data-manifest.md`.\n    80\t\n    81\t   Search goes inside binary files (`rg -a`, and SQL over every SQLite table for the ids). Its controls:\n    82\t   - a known hit: one of D613's 10 Step 1 reads in the week of 09-07;\n    83\t   - a known miss: one id written only into a scratch control file outside the searched roots.\n    84\t4. **Any read excludes the article, with one exception by actual use,** classified per folder and frozen before any hit is read:\n    85\t   - **The exception:** an old-system replay run folder and its byte copies, **only when** its decision record or run note shows it ran the\n    86\t     then-production configuration as the product (its library sealed or served as the product's), and no decision cites one of its articles as a\n    87\t     case behind a change.\n    88\t   - **Development replays are reads:** arms, probes, configuration trials and week tests.\n    89\t   - **Why:** a replay of the product as it ran tunes neither system. D665's own council used the sealed run as the old system's output.\n    90\t   - **Still a read:** any research artifact built from such a run (for example, the 2026-10-28 quality review).\n    91\t   - **Recorded:** the per-folder classification (`replay-folders.json`, with each folder's record) and the council decision on it, both before\n    92\t     the hits are read.\n    93\t5. **One input.**\n    94\t   - A filtered copy of the items database without the excluded articles is made once and hashed.\n    95\t   - **Code change A:** `run_fresh.py --items` hands it to both children (clean `--source`; production `--testdb`).\n    96\t   - **Code change B:** `fresh_week_inputs.py` takes the dates and the items path as arguments.\n    97\t   - **Proof through the real entry points:** both launchers run dry with fake answers over the whole span on the copy. Each records the\n    98\t     ordered ids it actually processes and their text sha. The two must be equal in order. Controls:\n    99\t     - an excluded article appears in neither;\n   100\t     - a kept article appears in both.\n   101\t\n   102\t### 1.3 Blind matter census (articles only; frozen before any send)\n   103\t\n   104\t1. **Sample (D668).** Every kept article in the scored window 2026-08-31 to 09-20, read in batches of about 40 per session. Each family merges across every batch and week before reconciling. The warm-up is 07-01 to 08-30, unfiltered: the exclusion filter applies to the scored window only. Read with week reading A1's\n   105\t   brief (`../2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/brief-A1.md`), unchanged except for the file names.\n   106\t2. **Readers.** Codex (memories off, closed, D661) and Claude (closed command line), apart. Nine weeks × 2 families, in fresh sessions.\n   107\t3. **Merge.** One matter is one bet. Two entries are one matter only when the same outcome settles both and their answers cannot move apart.\n   108\t   Another year of a figure is another matter.\n   109\t   - Each family merges its own nine lists in one session.\n   110\t   - Two reconcilers, one per family, map every matter across the two families' lists (`same` or `none`). A matter is listed by both when\n   111\t     both reconcilers say `same`.\n   112\t   - A one-family matter goes to two third readers (one per family) with its articles. It joins the list when both say it is worth a question.\n   113\t4. **The company-figure flag.** Each family marks every listed matter `company figure` or not, using D505's own list: 营收、毛利率的指引、市场预期、实际业绩.\n   114\t   The flag is set when both say yes; a split is listed.\n   115\t5. **N** is every listed matter (D665). The flagged subgroup is printed beside the verdict with its own a, b and N, because by D505 the new\n   116\t   system does not open such questions. Alex sees how much of any gap that rule makes. (D431: the comparison is of the products as\n   117\t   configured.)\n   118\t6. **De-duplication and freeze (D671).** Both families group the entries that are one bet (D248); a pair only one family grouped, or one a reviewer names, goes to one blind adjudicator of each family with its articles; a merge needs both families or both adjudicators. **Frozen:** `stage0/census/matters.json`, the only scoring list: each matter with one canonical question, its source articles, its first source day and its flag; its hash is in the versions manifest.\n   119\t7. **Too small at once:** if N < 20, the verdict is \"too small\" and goes to Alex before any paid send.\n   120\t\n   121\t### 1.4 The verdict rule (`verdict-rule.json`, hashed; `score_comparison.py` with a test and a mutant per rule)\n   122\t\n   123\t1. **The cutoff:** one for every matter, the end of the invocation's last day (09-20 in the full run). Libraries are read as they stand then.\n   124\t   - Closed questions still count.\n   125\t   - Timeliness is not scored. The days from first source to opening are printed beside.\n   126\t2. **Covers (mapping, A2's form, blind):**\n   127\t   - **The rule:** both families mark the question `opened`, or `narrower` with both saying it carries the matter's main outcome\n   128\t     (ACCEPTANCE-DESIGN A; `tally_week.covered`). `broader` and `missed` do not cover.\n   129\t   - **Blinding:** the readers see each draw's library under a random X/Y label, the key outside the repository, and are told to ignore\n   130\t     style.\n   131\t   - **Disagreements:** two third readers, one per family. A remaining split is resolved per counting (item 7, order: covers > does not\n   132\t     cover).\n   133\t3. **Same question:**\n   134\t   - **A question** is an id in a system-draw's library at the cutoff, the system's own identity. Distinct questions are distinct ids.\n   135\t   - **Matter to question:** the mapping rule above.\n   136\t   - **Question to question:** two questions of one library are the same bet when the same outcome settles both and their answers cannot move\n   137\t     apart (A1's rule; D248). Of such a pair, **the earlier-opened question is the original and the later one the duplicate** (as\n   138\t     `week/brief-A2.md` named the order).\n   139\t   - **During mapping:** the readers, who read each whole library, also mark its duplicates.\n   140\t   - **Distinct bets** are the ids less the duplicates both families mark. The 30-question minimum counts distinct bets. A split duplicate is\n   141\t     counted both ways, and the minimum must hold both ways.\n   142\t   - **On cards:** a duplicate stays a question in the worth guardrail.\n   143\t4. **The cards and their marks:**\n   144\t   - **Worth tracking:** worth, trivia or duplicate. Each card lists its library's other questions in opening order, with their opening days.\n   145\t     `duplicate` is marked only on the later question of a same-bet pair.\n   146\t   - **Answer:** supported, unsupported, insufficient, or no answer (a question not judged by the cutoff).\n   147\t   - **Cannot tell:** a split after the debate rounds, resolved in each counting by item 7.\n   148\t5. **Served in a draw:** at least one covering question is marked `worth` and supported, in each counting after item 7's resolution. A matter **served by a system** is served in\n   149\t   at least 2 of its 3 draws.\n   150\t6. **The quantities:** a = served only by the new system; b = served only by the old; N = all listed matters.\n   151\t7. **The two countings.** A split (\"cannot tell\" after the debate rounds) resolves to one of the marks its readers actually gave.\n   152\t   - **Counting 1:** the new system's cards take the most favourable mark given; the old system's cards take the least favourable.\n   153\t   - **Counting 2:** the reverse.\n   154\t   - **The order:** for the mapping, covers > does not cover; for the answer, supported > insufficient > unsupported; for worth, worth >\n   155\t     duplicate or trivia.\n   156\t   - **How a resolved mark counts:** it counts as that mark. A resolved supported or unsupported mark enters the decided denominators. A\n   157\t     resolved insufficient mark stays out of them, as any insufficient mark does.\n   158\t   - A guardrail subgroup is **exercised** only with at least 10 cards decided **without** a split. A subgroup that is not exercised cannot\n   159\t     breach. It blocks only a practical lead; a loss established otherwise still stands.\n   160\t8. **The guardrails** (D428's measure, which D665 turns into these guardrails):\n   161\t   - **The sample:** the random guardrail sample only, 35 questions per system-draw by seed 20261117, or all of them when a library holds\n   162\t     fewer. Covering cards are not in these shares. The three draws are pooled per system.\n   163\t   - **The shares:**\n   164\t     - the share of **decided** figure-question answers (supported or unsupported) that are unsupported;\n   165\t     - the same for yes/no questions;\n   166\t     - the share of decided worth marks that are trivia or duplicate.\n   167\t   - **Reported beside, not in the shares:** insufficient and no answer, with the number of splits and how each counting resolved them.\n   168\t   - **The bar:** the new system's share may not be more than 10 points above the old system's.\n   169\t   - **Exercised:** each subgroup needs at least 10 cards per system decided without a split (item 7), or it is \"not exercised\".\n   170\t9. **The controls,** outside every product count:\n   171\t   - 6 planted wrong answers (a rung moved, 3 per system). Each needs an explicit unsupported or insufficient mark, or the reading is invalid.\n   172\t   - 4 supported controls, confirmed first by a separate reader.\n   173\t   - 3 hidden repeats.\n   174\t10. **No release verdict without six complete draws.** A draw that cannot finish (stopped after D449's one redo, or by its guard) gives no\n   175\t    release verdict. What finished goes to Alex as a diagnostic, with the cause.\n   176\t    - **D431's \"same day\" for a run longer than one day:** a new-system draw takes about 20 h, so one calendar day cannot hold it.\n   177\t      - The six counted draws are launched within one hour of each other and run concurrently.\n   178\t      - A D449 redo inside a draw counts as part of that draw.\n   179\t      - Every counted send falls within 72 h of the first launch.\n   180\t      - A breach makes the result a diagnostic.\n   181\t      - Recorded as decision D667 before any paid send, replacing D431's one-calendar-day reading for this run only.\n   182\t    - No replacement draw counts toward the verdict. The reviewers read \"same day\" differently (Codex: the send day; the Claude reviewer:\n   183\t      the window), so the council took the cheaper, reversible course (D439): a later replacement would be a diagnostic only.\n   184\t11. **The verdicts, tested in this order:**\n   185\t    1. **Invalid:** a plant without an explicit rejection, fewer than 3 of 4 supported controls accepted, or fewer than 2 of 3 repeats marked\n   186\t       the same.\n   187\t    2. **Too small:** N < 20, or fewer than 30 distinct questions in any system-draw.\n   188\t    3. **Practical lead:** a − b ≥ max(3, ⌈0.15·N⌉) and a ≥ 2b under both countings, with every guardrail exercised and passing under both.\n   189\t    4. **Practical loss:** b − a ≥ max(3, ⌈0.15·N⌉) and b ≥ 2a under both countings (needing no guardrail exercised), or a breach in an\n   190\t       exercised guardrail under both.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n     2\t\n     3\t> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n     4\t> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n     5\t> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n     6\t> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n     7\t> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n     8\t> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n     9\t> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n    10\t> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n    13\t\n    14\tBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n    15\t\n    16\t1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n    17\t2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n    18\t3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n    19\t   word for it is already given.\n    20\t4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n    21\t   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n    22\t5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n    23\t   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n    24\t\n    25\t## 1. Goal (end to end)\n    26\t\n    27\tEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\n    28\tnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\n    29\tany one step's own score.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n    32\t\n    33\t**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n    34\t\n    35\tThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n    36\t\n    37\t- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n    38\t  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n    39\t  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n    40\t  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n    41\t  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n    42\t- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n    43\t  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n    44\t  nothing at all and were never judged.\n    45\t\n    46\t**Evidence:**\n    47\t\n    48\t| Evidence | Content | Source |\n    49\t|---|---|---|\n    50\t| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n    51\t| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n    52\t| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n    53\t| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n    54\t\n    55\tAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n    58\t\n    59\t| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n    60\t|---|---|---|\n    61\t| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n    62\t| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n    63\t| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n    64\t| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n    65\t| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n    66\t| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n    67\t| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n    68\t| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n    69\t\n    70\t## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n    71\t\n    72\t- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n    73\t  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n    74\t  system (D263).\n    75\t- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n    76\t  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n    77\t  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n    78\t- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n    79\t- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n    80\t  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n    81\t  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n    82\t  competition or pricing\".\n    83\t- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n    84\t  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n    85\t  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n    86\t  of the previous filing topic).\n    87\t- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n    88\t  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n    89\t  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n    90\t  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n    91\t- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n    92\t\n    93\t## 5. What to do\n    94\t\n    95\tStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n    96\t\n    97\t1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n    98\t   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n    99\t   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   100\t   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n   101\t     the existing rule).\n   102\t   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   103\t   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   104\t   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   105\t   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n   106\t     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n   107\t2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   108\t   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   109\t   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   110\t   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   111\t   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   112\t   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   113\t   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   114\t   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n   115\t3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   116\t   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n   117\t4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   118\t   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n   119\t\n   120\t## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n   121\t\n   122\t- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n   123\t  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n   124\t  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n   125\t- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n   126\t  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n   127\t  measured and reported apart.\n   128\t- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n   129\t- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n   130\t  counted after.\n   131\t- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n   132\t  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n   133\t  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n   134\t  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n   135\t    question\";\n   136\t  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n   137\t  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n   138\t  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n   139\t    or no question uses);\n   140\t  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n   141\t  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n   142\t    ones that matter one by one.\n   143\t- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n   144\t  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n   145\t  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n   146\t  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n   147\t    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n   148\t    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n   149\t      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n   150\t    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n   151\t      facts must not fall.\n   152\t  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n   153\t    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n   154\t      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n   155\t      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n   156\t      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n   157\t    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n   158\t      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n   159\t    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n   160\t      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n   161\t      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n   162\t      what was measured.\n   163\t  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n   164\t    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n   165\t  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n   166\t  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n   167\t\n   168\t## 7. Process (no drift)\n   169\t\n   170\t- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n   171\t  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n   172\t  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n   173\t- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n   174\t- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n   175\t  sentence, in both directions.\n   176\t- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n   177\t- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n   178\t\n   179\t## 8. Steps and where we are\n   180\t\n   181\t| # | Step | Output | Status |\n   182\t|---|---|---|---|\n   183\t| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n   184\t| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n   185\t| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n   186\t| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n   187\t| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n   188\t| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n   189\t| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n   190\t| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n   191\t| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n   192\t\n   193\t## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n   194\t\n   195\t- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n   196\t- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n   197\t- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n   198\t- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n   199\t- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n   200\t- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n   201\t- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/DESIGN-1.6.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md | tail -125'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/DESIGN-1.6.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md | tail -125'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The §1.6 tools: what D665 prints beside the verdict (design v7, before any output is read; D672)\n     2\t\n     3\tGoverning text:\n     4\t- `RUN-PLAN-v12.md` §1.6;\n     5\t- `stage0/verdict-rule.json`: `hard_checks` and `printed_beside`.\n     6\t\n     7\tThe ruler and the frozen instruments do not move (D674). Every reader is a closed session (`census/census_readers.run`), one per\n     8\tfamily, with memories off (D661).\n     9\t\n    10\tv2 answered round 4's Part B and v3 round 5's. v4 and v5 answer round 6: Codex in `tools-review-codex-6.md`, and a Claude reader. Every\n    11\tround's Part B points are summarised in D675.\n    12\t\n    13\t## 0. Inputs, release status and the report\n    14\t\n    15\t- **Stores.**\n    16\t  - Every tool reads a draw's components from its `run.json`: `components[c]['folder']`, so `step1-redo` or `step2-redo` after a D449\n    17\t    redo, and the judge's completed attempt (`mapping.store_paths`).\n    18\t  - `eligibility.py` fingerprints every file a tool reads: the judge or old store, `step1/trace.sqlite`, `step2/decisions.json` and\n    19\t    `library.json`, `cases/cases.json`, and each pass's ladder `outcomes.jsonl`.\n    20\t  - Each tool refuses a file whose sha256 differs.\n    21\t- **Release status sits beside the verdict and does not replace it.**\n    22\t  - `score_comparison` gives the verdict (invalid / too small / lead / loss / no winner).\n    23\t  - The report prints \"hard checks clear\" when every hard check passed. It prints \"release blocked: <check> failed\" for a check that\n    24\t    failed, and \"release not cleared: <check> not measured\" for a check that could not be measured (D665: \"a release blocker reported\n    25\t    with the verdict\").\n    26\t  - Release itself still needs a practical lead and Alex's word. Nothing is deployed.\n    27\t- **A hard check that is not measured cannot clear release.**\n    28\t  - The frozen rule says this of Step 2's lines. This design reads it the same way for D400's code parts, whose own accounting marks a\n    29\t    line \"not measured\" below 3 company-figure theses or 20 laddered theses.\n    30\t  - This is a reading to settle in the council and record before any output; it is the cautious, reversible course.\n    31\t- **Printed beside, each from its tool:**\n    32\t\n    33\t| Item | Tool | Source |\n    34\t|---|---|---|\n    35\t| D428's limits beside the guardrails | `score_comparison.beside` | Labelled as a comparison on the guardrail sample, not Stage B's measurement. Each system's figure share against 12.7% to 14.4%; its yes/no share against 5%; its decided latest answers (figure and yes/no) unsupported against 3 in 30, scaled; with denominators, and the insufficient latest answers beside |\n    36\t| Sign test and interval (illustrative) | `score_comparison.beside` | Per counting, over the discordant matters (served by exactly one system): two-sided binomial p on a against b, and the Wilson 95% interval for the new system's share among them, labelled illustrative |\n    37\t| Company-figure subgroup a, b, N | `score_comparison.beside` | Built |\n    38\t| Days from first source to opening | `score_comparison.beside` | For each matter and draw with a confirmed covering question (split covers not counted): the earliest publication day among the matter's `articles` (the census numbering), to the earliest opening day of a covering question. A question opened before 09-07 is printed \"opened in the warm-up\"; one opened in the window before that day, \"opened before the first listed article\" |\n    39\t| Floors | `score_comparison.beside` | §4 |\n    40\t| D400 reader lines, D400 code parts | §1 | |\n    41\t| Step 2's three lines | §2 | |\n    42\t| D431's 07-14 reading | §3 | |\n    43\t| 37 bulk-viewer articles excluded; 2 split matter pairs kept apart | report text | D669 and D671 records |\n    44\t\n    45\t## 1. D400, all three passes\n    46\t\n    47\t- **Code parts** (`d400_code_check.py`): Stage A's accounting, line 6, on each new draw's three ladder passes. The ladder folder comes\n    48\t  from `run.json`, and every `outcomes.jsonl` is checked against its fingerprint in `eligibility.json`.\n    49\t  - A skipped ladder component (no question opened) is \"not measured\".\n    50\t  - Stored two-week runs (c1 to c3) could not measure the due-day line (7 to 11 theses with three ladders, 20 needed). The full window\n    51\t    has about 8 times the articles.\n    52\t  - \"Figure ranges meet end to end\" is enforced at the write (`ladder_step.check_answer`).\n    53\t- **Reader lines 1–5:** Stage A's reading, unchanged, run per draw. Line 5's minimums scale as D452 scaled them\n    54\t  (`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/READING-PLAN.md`): ceil(35N/44) right and ceil(28N/44) right by a ladder. D400's 35 of 44 and the\n    55\t  actual N are printed beside.\n    56\t  - `d400_reader_lines.py` recomputes line 5 from `collect_readings.py report`'s counts (right, right by a ladder, of N) with the\n    57\t    scaled minimums. `accounting.py` hard-codes 35 and 28, so its own line 5 is not read. N = 0 reads \"not measured\" (D452). `collect_readings.py` reads one ladder folder and its packet files:\n    58\t  `build_reader_packets.py <key> --run <ladder folder>=<cases.json> --readers <draw's reader folder>`, then\n    59\t  `collect_readings.py rows | disputes | report` on that folder.\n    60\t\n    61\t## 2. Step 2's three lines on draw new-1 (hard)\n    62\t\n    63\tInputs: new-1's Step 1 trace, its `decisions.json` and its `library.json`. The seed is 20261117 plus the line's number.\n    64\t\n    65\t**Line 1, search: recall over known labelled counterparts, not over every real repeat** (as the Step 2 log §12 labels it).\n    66\t- **Sample:** records are drawn in rounds, one at random from each of three equal article strata (by forward position) per round, until\n    67\t  100 have a labelled counterpart or the strata are exhausted. The first 100 labelled records in draw order are the sample.\n    68\t- **Candidates:** earlier records only, in forward order, from two routes that do not use Step 2's embeddings:\n    69\t  - entity-wide word overlap, top 10, scored by `views/matching._words` directly, as R0e scores it (no embedding fetch);\n    70\t  - the same original party within 3 days.\n    71\t- **Labels:** two readers, one per family, answer the neutral relation question of the Step 2 reference labelling\n    72\t  (`poc/data/step2-validation-2026-11-07/v2-prompt-*.md`, question 1: the relation of the later record to the earlier, with no claim that\n    73\t  the system filed it). Each disagreement goes to one more closed session, whose family is drawn per pair by seed, so that no family\n    74\t  settles every dispute. A counterpart is a pair labelled `same_statement`, as `score_final.py` counts it.\n    75\t- **Generator-miss audit:** the Step 2 log's whole-history scan (`build_reader_packets.py scan`), run on a fixed subset of 12 sampled\n    76\t  records: the first 4 drawn in each stratum. Each record is shown in full beside every earlier record's summary, chunked.\n    77\t  - Each record the scan flags is a candidate, not a label. It goes through the same full-context labelling and adjudication as the\n    78\t    routes' candidates before it can count.\n    79\t  - The audit's yield is reported: the counterparts the routes missed.\n    80\t- **The labels are frozen** (file and sha256) before Step 2's candidates are read.\n    81\t- **Pass:** a group holding a labelled counterpart is among the record's Step 2 candidates (`decisions.json` `candidates`) for at least 90\n    82\t  of 100 labelled records. Fewer than 100: **not measured**.\n    83\t\n    84\t**Line 2, clear wrong merges.**\n    85\t- **Population:** every repeat in `decisions.json` that was not refused, each joined to its recorded `repeat_of` original.\n    86\t- **Sample:** 200 at random.\n    87\t- **Reading:** the frozen Step 2 merge reading (`poc/data/clean-step2-forward-2026-11-08/merge-prompt-1.md`, and `merge-third-prompt.md`\n    88\t  from the same folder on every pair either reader rejected).\n    89\t  The packet goes in the prompt in place of the file path.\n    90\t- **Pass:** the third reader's `wrong` count is at most 4 of 200. Fewer than 200 repeats: **not measured**.\n    91\t\n    92\t**Line 3, later details: the shipped renderer, as the Step 2 log §12 measured it.**\n    93\t- **Population:** groups whose original has at least one later report.\n    94\t- **Sample:** 30 at random.\n    95\t- **The two renderings:**\n    96\t  - \"grouped\": the frozen dossier, `step3/library.group_text(member_text=True)`;\n    97\t  - \"apart\": each member as its own entry, `record_text`.\n    98\t- **An independent detail key, of content only** (the council's round-8 consensus, D675):\n    99\t  - **A detail** is what a later report's statement adds to its group's original: \"a figure, condition, party, date or qualification an\n   100\t    analyst would want to keep\" (the Step 2 reference's question 2; PRD §6, \"details a later report adds, such as a guidance\n   101\t    condition\"; the recorded test's \"detail\" category).\n   102\t  - The record's own attribution fields (tier, stated day, period, reporting speaker) are not shown to the key readers. The publisher\n   103\t    and publication date are shown as context only, and the prompts say they are never details (`CONTENT_ONLY`). A party or period\n   104\t    that changes the claim still counts when the statement's own text says it.\n   105\t  - Two closed key readers, one per family, each list the details. A third session settles every detail they do not share.\n   106\t  - The key is frozen before either rendering is read.\n   107\t- **Reading:**\n   108\t  - Each rendering of a group goes to its own closed session in each family: four sessions per group. No session sees both renderings,\n   109\t    as in the recorded test, where each reader read one view.\n   110\t  - Both renderings carry the same surrounding dossier: the other groups under the same thesis, each as its original's record.\n   111\t  - The session marks which key details its rendering states.\n   112\t- **Pass:** over the 30 groups, the grouped rendering's detail total is no fewer than the apart rendering's, summed over both families,\n   113\t  as the recorded test summed both readers. Each family's totals are printed beside.\n   114\t  - The mean paired difference per group is printed with a 95% bootstrap interval over groups.\n   115\t  - Fewer than 30 groups: **not measured**.\n   116\t- **Printed beside:** the count of later reports whose tier, stated day, period or speaker differs from the original's (the fields\n   117\t  `group_text` drops), counted by code.\n   118\t- **Disclosed with the result:** both renderings carry the same content, so the line checks that grouping loses no content a later\n   119\t  report adds, against the readers' own noise. It is weaker than the recorded test's unprompted questions.\n   120\t\n   121\tLines 1 and 2 are decided on their point estimates, with a Wilson 95% interval printed beside.\n   122\t\n   123\t## 3. D431's frozen-reference reading at 07-14 (printed beside; both systems)\n   124\t\n   125\tD442's procedure (`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/DESIGN.md`, \"Procedure\", steps 3 to 6) is run as it was run then, on both\n   126\tsystems' draws as they stood at 07-14. It compares the answers the systems actually gave; MATCHING-v2 §4's adapter, which would re-ask\n   127\tthe ruler's question, is not built (D442's precedent).\n   128\t- **The as-of state:** every view created on or before 2026-07-14 is listed first: by `created_seq` up to the last sequence of 07-14,\n   129\t  closed views included. Then `service.state_as_of` at 2026-07-14 is read for each one. New-system units are joined by\n   130\t  `clean_ladders.view_id`, split children included.\n   131\t- **Step 4, matching:** MATCHING-v2's SAME rule, two blind families, and two third readers, one per family, on each disagreement, as the\n   132\t  full run did (`READING-COMMITMENT.md`). A split stays unresolved. D442's rule for a ruler question's candidates applies.\n   133\t- **Step 5, eligibility:** question, period and quantity the ruler's, and due day the ruler's (D430). Decided by two blind readers, with\n   134\t  two third readers, one per family, on a disagreement; a split stays unresolved. The literal reading (the same due day) is printed beside.\n   135\t- **Step 6, outcomes:** the first that applies, (i) to (ix).\n   136\t  - Several SAME views: the first created is judged and the others listed.\n   137\t  - A figure rung with no bound: \"no comparison\". A unit that differs from the ruler question's, or that cannot be established:\n   138\t    \"no comparison (unit)\". No conversion is made.\n   139\t  - Agreement by the frozen scorer's rule (`prod_records.outcome` for the old system; for the new system the same rule, as Stage B counted\n   140\t    it: \"Ruled out\" and \"Still open\" answer no, `stage_b/control_theses.py`).\n   141\t- **Printed:** per ruler question and draw, its outcome; and per draw and system, the agreements on the seven graded questions.\n   142\t\n   143\t## 4. The floors (printed beside; proposed, not gates)\n   144\t\n   145\tAs their source states them (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-10/codex-1.md`; `RUN-PLAN-v2.md` §1.6). Each floor is\n   146\tcomputed per draw and under both countings, and holds when it is met in at least 2 of the system's 3 draws:\n   147\t- **Covered:** at least 80% of important matters have a covering question.\n   148\t- **Trivial or duplicate:** at most 20% of the questions, counted as decided worth marks on every random-sample card, covering cards\n   149\t  included. The frozen rule keeps covering cards out of the guardrail shares only.\n   150\t- **Supported:** at least 90% of latest answers, counted on every answered random-sample card. An insufficient or unresolved answer is\n   151\t  counted and gets no credit.\n   152\t\n   153\tWrong citations and answer-changing omissions: **not measured**.\n   154\t\n   155\t## Tables\n   156\t\n   157\t**Value × reader:**\n   158\t\n   159\t| Value | Written by | Read by |\n   160\t|---|---|---|\n   161\t| `eligibility.json` (all fingerprints) | eligibility | every §1.6 tool, export, scorer |\n   162\t| d400 code check | `d400_code_check` | report: release status |\n   163\t| d400 reader lines 1–5 | `d400_reader_lines.py` (Stage A's tools on verified copies) | report: printed beside only |\n   164\t| step2-lines.json | `step2_lines.py` | report: release status |\n   165\t| d431-0714.json | `d431_reading.py` | report |\n   166\t| beside quantities | `score_comparison.beside` | report |\n   167\t\n   168\t**State × event:**\n   169\t\n   170\t| Event | Handling |\n   171\t|---|---|\n   172\t| A reader session voids | Retried once, then reported missing. A line with a missing mark is not measured |\n   173\t| A draw stopped or ineligible | No release verdict. `eligibility.py` still records every finished draw's fingerprints and names the draws that stop the aggregate. The tools run on what finished, as a diagnostic (plan §1.4 item 10) |\n   174\t| A file changed after eligibility | The tool refuses |\n   175\t| A component was redone | Its counted folder is the one `run.json` names |\n    33\t| 22:41 | reading tools | mapping.py (3 tests), mark_cards.py (4 tests), build_cards.py type field; card form check (D673): round 1 named two rung annotations (removed) and a truncation artefact of the check itself; round 2 named only the systems' own structure (rung counts, library sizes), disclosed | stage0/blinding-check-codex*.md |\n    34\t| 23:03 | check | six draws running; spend new 0.43–0.45, old 0.91–1.08; my own mistake: a status check tried to count rows in a live Step 1 trace (AGENTS §9: never open a live run's databases); refused by the classifier, not repeated; status is read from run.json and the ledgers only | run/ |\n    35\t| 23:24 | card form check (D673), rounds 3–4 on the new card (request context, own opening place, numbered answers) | round 3 named one form element: the answer step's third annotation form ('the amount last reported when the question opened'); the annotation filter is now the closed set of ladder_step's three suffixes (484 stored conditions, 0 left annotated). Round 4 named only content (rung counts, theme counts, library size): closed, disclosed with the result | stage0/blinding-check-codex-3.md, -4.md |\n    36\t| 23:37 | reading-tools council, rounds 2–4 | round 2–3 both readers not ready (eligibility, mapping validation, publishers, debate, plants, split readings, card context); round 4: Claude ready to read, Codex 3 more (key-verified card flags, reconciled attempt log, theme rungs), fixed; 47 tests, 39 mutants + 5 controls as predicted; §1.6 design v2 sent with round 5 | D675; tools-review-codex-2..4.md; DESIGN-1.6.md |\n    37\t| 00:06 | §1.6 design rounds 5–7 → v6; reading tools committed (c3704776, 802f2b08) | both readers: design faithful after v6's small fixes; built eligibility inputs, D400 code check, printed-beside quantities, diagnostic export (54 tests, 43 mutants + 6 controls); three builders started in parallel for D400 reader lines, Step 2 lines, D431 at 07-14 (new files only, fake readers) | DESIGN-1.6.md v6; D675 |\n    38\t| 00:29 | three §1.6 tools built (D400 reader lines 7 tests, Step 2 lines 10, D431 6; each with mutants on scratch copies) | finding: Step 2 line 3 as v6 defines it is predetermined (group_text drops later reports' tier/stated day/period/speaker; v6 counts them; c1: 78/79 groups) while the product's judge reads each member's full record (PRD §6; 741 and 1,127 same-claim notes in c1/p1 requests); sent to the council as round 8 with the tools | tools-review-brief-8.md |\n    39\t| 01:02 | §1.6 tools pass the council (round 10, Codex READY; Claude round 9 points fixed) | every instrument D672 requires is built and reviewed before any output: reading tools (round 5) and §1.6 tools (round 10); 92 stage0 tests; CURRENT_STATE next task = the reading order once the six draws end | f2cbfcfa |\n    40\t| 01:20 | old-1, old-2 complete | old-1 $9.83, old-2 $8.60, no stop; old-3 near the end; the new draws still in Step 1 (about $2.8 each) | run/ |\n    41\t| 01:21 | old-3 complete | $8.83, no stop; the three old draws total $27.26 against $25 guards each (not a budget); the new draws are in Step 1 | run/ |\n    42\t| 13:11 | reading chain dry run end to end on the slice ($0, fake reader) | every step joins; verdict 'too small' as ordered; gap fixed: printed-beside quantities now computed for invalid and too-small verdicts too | stage0/chain_dry_run.py |\n    43\t\n    44\t- 2026-11-18 13:59: The scorer and the chain rebuild were hardened through Codex rounds 11–22. Round 22 returned READY.\n    45\t  - The verdict is computed only on a snapshot rebuilt from the primary data.\n    46\t  - Any derived file that differs from the rebuild is refused.\n    47\t  - Coverage: 101 stage0 tests; 75 mutants, all as predicted; the chain dry run passes (D675).\n    48\t  - The run is unchanged: old-1..3 finished; new-1..3 are in Step 1, at $16.2–17.0 each at 13:39.\n    49\t\n    50\t- 2026-11-18 18:12: new-2 finished Step 1 after 19 h 36 min. Its step1.log reads `status complete, error null, calls 1899`, and $21.14 was spent.\n    51\t  Step 2 then started at 18:12:37. Two figures to keep apart: `reserved_usd` 265.30 in the log is the running sum of what each call set\n    52\t  aside before it ran, while the ledger's spend is $21.14. new-1 and new-3 are still in Step 1.\n    53\t\n    54\t- 2026-11-18 19:00: new-2 finished Step 2 (18:12–19:00) and wrote its cases.\n    55\t  - Step 2: 1473 articles, 5256 records, 2327 groups. Outcomes: 2181 new, 2929 repeat, 146 revision; none refused. Later linking judged\n    56\t    26647 and linked 205.\n    57\t  - Cases: the trace opened 60, and all 60 were written.\n    58\t  - The ladder started at 19:00:09. Spent so far: $25.94.\n    59\t\n    60\t- 2026-11-18 19:04: **new-1 Step 1 stopped, `complete_with_failures`.**\n    61\t  - 1890 calls, $22.03. One manifest item failed: gnews:24da4cc269dbf1a4b9fdf76b. On the send and again on its own retry, the answer\n    62\t    left out the required `figures` field of evidence items 2 to 4 (`SchemaError`).\n    63\t  - The draw is now awaiting a redo decision.\n    64\t  - This is an answer that does not fit its schema after its retry, so D449 makes it eligible for a redo, and plan §3 allows one per\n    65\t    component.\n    66\t- 2026-11-18 19:05: **new-1 Step 1 redo launched,** with the reason recorded, through `launch_comparison.py`.\n    67\t  - The launch checks passed: no uncommitted code, versions manifest unchanged, guard $65.\n    68\t  - New pid 92883, folder `step1-redo`, running from empty.\n    69\t  - **Projection:** the first attempt cost $22.03. The redo should cost about $21, then Step 2 about $5, the ladder about $7, and the\n    70\t    judge $5 to $10. That puts new-1 at about $60 to $65, at its $65 guard. If the guard stops it, the draw gives no release verdict\n    71\t    (plan §1.4 item 10).\n    72\t  - **Timing:** expected to finish by about 15:00 to 17:00 on 11-19, inside D667's 72-hour window, which closes 2026-11-20 22:36.\n    73\t- 2026-11-18 19:05: **The Mac is on battery,** at 12% with about 40 minutes left. Four draws are live: new-1 redo, new-2 judge, new-3\n    74\t  Step 1.\n    75\t  - If the machine dies, the launchers die and the draws stop.\n    76\t  - Alex was notified to plug in the charger. A power monitor is armed.\n    77\t- 2026-11-18 19:06: AC power back (13%, charging); no draw lost. The power monitor stays on for source changes.\n    78\t- 2026-11-18 ~20:10: new-3 Step 1 ended with `status complete`, `error None`, 1892 calls. Step 2 started. new-2's judge stands at $33.28 so far: $0.91 in its first 66 minutes.\n    79\t- 2026-11-18 20:56: **new-2 complete**, at $34.66 in all.\n    80\t  - By component: Step 1 $21.14; Step 2 about $4.80; the ladder about $6.40; the judge about $2.30, against the plan's $5 to $10.\n    81\t  - Revised projection for new-1, which pays for the Step 1 redo: about $22.0 + $21.2 + $4.8 + $6.4 + $2.3 = about $57. That is under\n    82\t    its $65 guard, with about $8 to spare.\n    83\t- 2026-11-18 21:03: new-3 Step 2 and cases done; ladder started ($27.76). Step 2: groups 2324; outcomes {'new': 2193, 'repeat': 2956, 'revision': 131}; refused 0; several same_statement answers 1306; later linking: judged 28281, linked 135, pending 0. Cases: new-3: 60 cases written to poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/cases/cases.json (the trace opened 60)\n    84\t- 2026-11-18 21:02: Mac unplugged at 100%. Alex was notified to keep it on power overnight; new-1's redo needs about 18 h.\n    85\t- 2026-11-18 22:57: **new-3 complete**, at $37.64 in all.\n    86\t  - Step 1 $21.3; Step 2 and the cases about $6.4; the ladder about $6.9; the judge about $3.0.\n    87\t  - Five of the six draws are complete. new-1's Step 1 redo, launched at 19:05, is still running.\n    88\t  - Eligibility and the reading wait for all six draws, so nothing is read before then.\n    89\t- 2026-11-19 00:54: AC power back at 39% and charging, after about 4 h on battery. No draw was lost; new-1's Step 1 redo continues ($27.8).\n    90\t- 2026-11-19 10:55: **Alex:** \"i think you are really wasting my time and money with my reruns, just get it done and tell me the results\".\n    91\t  - Asked whether to wait for new-1 (about 4 h, about $17) or stop it and score the five finished draws. **He chose: \"Let it finish, then score\".**\n    92\t  - No new reruns.\n    93\t  - `reading-chain.sh` started detached (pid in `poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-chain.pid`). It waits for new-1 to complete, then\n    94\t    runs the CURRENT_STATE order: eligibility, then mapping, then cards, then the verdict, then the release checks. Outputs go to\n    95\t    `poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading/`.\n    96\t\n    97\t- 2026-11-19 12:27: **new-1's Step 1 redo stopped**, again `complete_with_failures`. It failed on the same single item\n    98\t  (gnews:24da4cc269dbf1a4b9fdf76b), with the same `SchemaError` (`figures` missing on evidence items 2 to 4), on the send and on its retry.\n    99\t  - Under D449 this second stop ends the draw: new-1 is `stopped`, and its ledger stopped at $43.36.\n   100\t  - There is **no release verdict** (plan §1.4 item 10). The reading chain exited unread.\n   101\t  - **My mistake:** the failure was reproducible, and I relaunched a full Step 1 (about 17 h and about $21) without first re-sending\n   102\t    that one stored request, which is a cent and a few seconds (AGENTS §7.3, \"a model call is a sampler, re-send before calling it a\n   103\t    defect\"; small first). Re-sending it would have shown that the redo could not pass.\n   104\t- 2026-11-19 12:30: **diagnostic reading of the five finished draws started** (`diagnostic-chain.sh`, pid in\n   105\t  `diagnostic-chain.pid`), as the plan directs: \"what finished goes to Alex as a diagnostic, with the cause\".\n   106\t  - Same tools, with the export's `--diagnostic` mode, and a labelled diagnostic count: a matter is served when it is served in 2 of the\n   107\t    system's finished draws.\n   108\t  - Readers are the codex and claude command-line tools. No system is rerun.\n   109\t- 2026-11-19 12:36: **Alex:** \"just look at this single article what the fuck is wrong and just do it individually\".\n   110\t  - **What was wrong.** At step1-redo sequence 365 the model wrote 5 evidence items. For items 2 to 4, which are qualitative and carry no\n   111\t    number, it left out the `figures` key, where the schema requires `figures: []`. Item 1 had no number either and did write `[]`.\n   112\t    - The other new draws hit the same thing: new-2 failed the first send identically and passed on its retry; new-3 passed first time.\n   113\t    - It is a sampler failure: new-1 failed 4 sends of 4.\n   114\t  - **What was done** (`single_item.py`):\n   115\t    - The item was sent alone through Step 1's own `run`, with the open theses it met (that row's `before_state`). The request was\n   116\t      byte-identical to the stored one. It passed first time. It opened 0 theses, and its `after_state` equals row 366's\n   117\t      `before_state`, so every later row stays valid.\n   118\t    - The send was charged on `ledgers/new-1-item.json` ($2 ceiling).\n   119\t    - It was spliced into a copy, `new-1/step1-patched/trace.sqlite`, which now has 1884 of 1884 rows complete. The originals are kept.\n   120\t    - Backups were made of `run.json` and the ledger (`*.bak-20261119-before-single-item`).\n   121\t    - The ledger stop was cleared, Step 1 marked completed in `step1-patched`, and new-1 relaunched normally at 12:40. Step 2 started.\n   122\t    - **This departs from D449's whole-component redo, on Alex's instruction.**\n   123\t  - **The release chain** (`reading-chain.sh`) was restarted and waits for new-1. Before its deck it sets the diagnostic deck key aside.\n   124\t- 2026-11-19 ~13:40: **DIAGNOSTIC result on the five finished draws** (`reading-diagnostic/diagnostic-count.json`; not a release verdict).\n   125\t  - **Controls:** all pass. 6/6 plants caught, 4/4 supported controls supported, 3/3 repeats the same.\n   126\t  - **Served matters (N = 32, margin 5):**\n   127\t    | | new | old | a (new only) | b (old only) |\n   128\t    |---|---|---|---|---|\n   129\t    | Counting 1 | 3 | 10 | 0 | 7 |\n   130\t    | Counting 2 | 2 | 10 | 0 | 8 |\n   131\t  - **Best case for the new system if new-1 served every matter served in new-2 or new-3:** new 4–5, old 10, a = 1, b = 6–7. That is\n   132\t    still no lead for the new system, and b − a ≥ 5.\n   133\t  - **What the old system serves and the new one misses:** M04 hyperscaler capex, M06 FY27 data-center revenue, M10 and M11 FY28 margin\n   134\t    and revenue, M17 the Anthropic anchor investment, M18 the FY27 gross margin.\n   135\t  - **What the new system serves:** M13, M15, M31, plus M19 and M32 in one draw only.\n   136\t- 2026-11-19 12:55: **Alex chose \"Stop it now\"**: new-1 cannot change the conclusion.\n   137\t  - new-1's launcher was stopped by SIGTERM in Step 2. Its record shows `crashed`, at $45.29.\n   138\t  - The release chain was stopped too. No process is left.\n   139\t  - **Final paid spend:** about $145 in all.\n   140\t    - Old system: $27.26 for its three draws.\n   141\t    - New system: new-2 $34.66, new-3 $37.64, new-1 $45.29, plus the $0.12 single-item send.\n   142\t  - **The answer stands as the 5-draw diagnostic:** the new system loses (served: new 2–3, old 10).\n   143\t- 2026-11-19 ~13:30: **Why the new system loses.** This is the D674 round's diagnosis, $0, from stored traces and libraries.\n   144\t  - **The new libraries hold no question on the company's own figures.** Of their 33–41 questions, none is on revenue, data-center\n   145\t    revenue, gross margin or hyperscaler capex. They are deals, investments, shipments and regulators. The old libraries open FY27\n   146\t    revenue, FY28 revenue, FY27 data-center revenue, FY27 gross margin and hyperscaler capex from 07-02 to 07-08.\n   147\t  - **The new system extracts the evidence but files no question for it.** new-2's Step 1 extracted 642 evidence items on\n   148\t    revenue, margin or capex; 336 were linked elsewhere and 306 kept unlinked.\n   149\t  - **The cause is the prompt's rule, by decision:**\n   150\t    - D382 (Alex: \"我觉得财务类型不应该是thesis，而更多的是条件…\"): \"a company's own figures are conditions, not questions\".\n   151\t    - D384: \"opens only the big matters\", with class A, the main-driver quantity, left out under D382.\n   152\t    - The week acceptance (2026-09-22..28) already found the same thing: the clean path lacked FY27 and FY28 revenue and the FY28\n   153\t      margin, and its stored reasoning named this do-not-open sentence.\n   154\t  - **Size of the class.** 7 of the 32 matters are company figures (M06, M07, M09, M10, M11, M18, M30). The old system serves 5–6 of\n   155\t    them and the new system 0. That is 4 of the old system's 7–8 old-only matters.\n   156\t  - **Outside the company figures** the new system still trails: old serves 6 and new 3. It misses M04, hyperscaler capex (a shared\n   157\t    quantity, which D384 allows), M17, the Anthropic anchor investment (an obligation), and M19 in 2 of its draws.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
