{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\\\"matter\\\": \\\"M(12|17|19|20|25|31|32)\\\"' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L{3,5}-codex.txt; rg -n '.' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third -g '*M31*' -g '*M20*'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/diagnostic-chain.sh | sed -n '20,79p'; nl -ba poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/deck/C001.txt | head -24; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/census/census_merge.py | sed -n '1,34p'\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third/L5--M20--Q20--codex.json:1:{\"covers\": false, \"why\": \"Signing the broader financing arrangement does not establish that NVIDIA provided residual-value support, and NVIDIA could sign such support agreements without finalizing the entire arrangement. The texts also identify different financing partners.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third/L5--M31--Q1--codex.json:1:{\"covers\": false, \"why\": \"The question limits obligations to the new revenue-sharing AI-factory model. The matter asks for total guarantees and credit support for cloud or AI partners, which could include obligations outside that model.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third/L6--M31--Q2--claude.json:1:{\"covers\": true, \"why\": \"Both target the same FY2027 10-K figure: NVIDIA's total credit-support/backstop obligations to AI cloud partners. Capacity-purchase backstops are guarantees. The ≥$10B bin is coarse against $108.5B, but it is the same outcome.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third/L3--M31--Q1--claude.json:1:{\"covers\": true, \"why\": \"Both ask the dollar amount of NVIDIA's guarantee/credit-support obligations to AI cloud partners at FY2027 year-end per the 10-K. The question's 'new model' scope is a core part of the matter and carries its main outcome.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third/L6--M20--Q29--claude.json:1:{\"covers\": false, \"why\": \"The question asks whether final platform agreements are completed. The matter asks whether NVIDIA commits to residual-value support (up to $125B), which the article calls optional and per-deal. Agreements could close without that backstop being signed.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third/L5--M31--Q1--claude.json:1:{\"covers\": true, \"why\": \"Both ask the guarantee and credit-support obligations to AI cloud partners disclosed in NVIDIA's FY2027 10-K. The question's limit to the new revenue-sharing model and its coarser bins only narrow the wording; it carries the matter's main outcome.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third/L6--M31--Q2--codex.json:1:{\"covers\": false, \"why\": \"The question combines credit support with capacity-purchase obligations, while the matter asks for third-party guarantees and credit support. Capacity purchases can include obligations outside that guarantee total, so the amounts need not match.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third/L6--M20--Q29--codex.json:1:{\"covers\": false, \"why\": \"Final platform agreements can be completed without NVIDIA committing residual-value support, which the article describes as optional. The question therefore does not determine whether NVIDIA signs the specific support agreements.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third/L5--M20--Q20--claude.json:1:{\"covers\": false, \"why\": \"The question asks whether the broad $500B financing plan with Apollo/Blackstone gets signed. The matter is a specific up-to-$125B residual-value support commitment for Goldman/Blackstone partnerships. Signing the broad plan wouldn't settle that specific commitment.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/third/L3--M31--Q1--codex.json:1:{\"covers\": false, \"why\": \"The question limits commitments to those under the new revenue-sharing and credit-support model. The matter asks for total guarantees and credit support for AI cloud partners, which could include obligations outside that model.\"}\n    20\tfor n in 1 2 3; do python3 $S/mapping.py read $OUT/mapping-out; done\n    21\tfor n in 1 2 3; do python3 $S/mapping.py third $OUT/mapping-out; done\n    22\tpython3 $S/mapping.py result $OUT/mapping-out\n    23\t\n    24\tstep \"3. cards\"\n    25\tpython3 $S/mark_cards.py deck $OUT/mapping-out $OUT/deck\n    26\tfor n in 1 2 3; do [ -e $OUT/deck/round-0.done ] || python3 $S/mark_cards.py mark $OUT/deck; done\n    27\tfor r in 1 2; do\n    28\t  for n in 1 2 3; do [ -e $OUT/deck/round-$r.done ] || python3 $S/mark_cards.py debate $OUT/deck $r; done\n    29\tdone\n    30\tpython3 $S/mark_cards.py readings $OUT/mapping-out $OUT/deck\n    31\t\n    32\tstep \"4. diagnostic count\"\n    33\tpython3 - \"$OUT/deck/readings.json\" > $OUT/diagnostic-count.json <<'PY'\n    34\timport json, sys\n    35\tsys.path.insert(0, '/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0')\n    36\timport score_comparison as sc\n    37\tr = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))\n    38\tout = {'note': 'DIAGNOSTIC, not a release verdict: five finished draws (new-1 stopped after its one redo); a matter is served by a '\n    39\t               'system when it is served in at least 2 of that system\\'s finished draws (both of new-2 and new-3; 2 of 3 old)',\n    40\t       'draws': sorted(r['cards']), 'N': len(r['matters']),\n    41\t       'margin_rule': max(3, -(-15 * len(r['matters']) // 100))}\n    42\tfor counting in ('1', '2'):\n    43\t    served = sc.served_by(r, counting)\n    44\t    a, b = len(served['new'] - served['old']), len(served['old'] - served['new'])\n    45\t    out[f'counting {counting}'] = {'a (new only)': a, 'b (old only)': b, 'served by new': len(served['new']),\n    46\t                                   'served by old': len(served['old']), 'both': len(served['new'] & served['old']),\n    47\t                                   'served_new': sorted(served['new']), 'served_old': sorted(served['old'])}\n    48\tc = r['controls']\n    49\tout['controls'] = {'plants marked unsupported or insufficient': sum(m in ('unsupported', 'insufficient') for m in c['plants']),\n    50\t                   'plants': len(c['plants']), 'supported controls marked supported': sum(m == 'supported' for m in c['supported']),\n    51\t                   'supported controls': len(c['supported']), 'repeats the same': sum(c['repeats_same']), 'repeats': len(c['repeats_same'])}\n    52\tout['distinct bets per draw'] = {d: v['ids'] - v['duplicates_both'] for d, v in r['distinct'].items()}\n    53\tprint(json.dumps(out, indent=1))\n    54\tPY\n    55\techo \"DIAGNOSTIC COUNT WRITTEN: $OUT/diagnostic-count.json\"\n    56\tstep \"done\"\n     1\tQuestion: What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-08-06\n     3\tDue: 2027-02-28\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. FY2027 gross margin is at least 78%.\n     6\t2. FY2027 gross margin is at least 75% and below 78%.\n     7\t3. FY2027 gross margin is at least 72% and below 75%, including the mid-seventies guidance range.\n     8\t4. FY2027 gross margin is at least 68% and below 72%.\n     9\t5. FY2027 gross margin is below 68%.\n    10\t\n    11\tLatest answer at the cutoff: FY2027 gross margin is at least 72% and below 75%, including the mid-seventies guidance range.\n    12\tReason given: The reference is NVIDIA's stated FY2027 “mid-seventies” gross-margin guidance, which sits in rung 3. The implied FY2027 result is about 73.7%: reported first-half margins are 75%, followed by Q3 guidance of 74% and Q4 guidance of 71%–72%; weighting by quarterly revenue and carrying Q3's 12.3% quarter-over-quarter revenue growth forward for Q4 leaves the year below rung 2's 75% boundary and above rung 4's 72% boundary. Higher memory costs and supply pressure reinforce the guided decline, but the additional views do not provide a sufficiently specific FY2027 revision to move the estimate across a boundary.\n    13\t\n    14\tMaterial that answer was given (41 items, by day):\n    15\t1. (party_statement; publisher trefis.com; speaker Trefis analyst; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-06) NVIDIA's gross-margin guidance for fiscal 2027 was in the mid-seventies, according to Trefis.\n    16\t   Quote: Three things decide whether the price works. Gross margin, guided to the mid-seventies for fiscal 2027, has to survive the Vera Rubin transition.\n    17\t2. (fact; publisher 247wallst.com; speaker Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-13) NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin of 75.0%.\n    18\t   Quote: Non-GAAP gross margin of 75.0%\n    19\t3. (fact; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of 75.0%.\n    20\t   Quote: For the quarter, GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins were both 75.0%.\n    21\t4. (party_statement; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA said current Hopper shipments dilute corporate gross margins and its forward outlook includes no China Data Center compute revenue.\n    22\t   Quote: Current Hopper shipments are dilutive to corporate gross margins. And given ongoing geopolitical uncertainty, there is no China data center compute revenue in our forward outlook.\n    23\t5. (party_statement; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA forecast Q3 FY2027 GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of 73.5%–74.5%, Q4 FY2027 margins of 71%–72%, and FY2028 margins of 72%–73%.\n    24\t   Quote: For Q3, we expect GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to be 74% plus or minus 50 basis points. We expect margins to bottom in Q4 in the 71% to 72% range before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal year '28, as executed price increases take effect in Q1.\n     1\t\"\"\"The blind matter census after the batch reading (plan §1.3 items 3–7; D668). Every session is closed (census_readers.run).\n     2\t\n     3\t    python3 census_merge.py merge        # each family merges its own 11 batch lists into one, flagging company figures (D505's list)\n     4\t    python3 census_merge.py reconcile    # a reconciler of each family pairs the two families' matters that are one bet\n     5\t    python3 census_merge.py third        # a third reader of each family reads each one-family matter with its articles: worth a question?\n     6\t    python3 census_merge.py freeze       # matters.json: both-listed matters, and one-family matters both third readers accept; N\n     7\t    python3 census_merge.py dedup        # both families group the entries that are one bet (D248)\n     8\t    python3 census_merge.py adjudicate   # pairs only one family grouped, and pairs a reviewer named, read blind against their articles\n     9\t    python3 census_merge.py final        # matters.json: agreed groups plus adjudicated one-bet pairs; one canonical question each; N\n    10\t\n    11\tA pair is \"listed by both\" only when both reconcilers name it. A one-family matter joins only when both third readers say yes. The company\n    12\tfigure flag is set when both families' marks say yes; a split is listed. N < 20 is \"too small\" and goes to Alex before any paid send.\n    13\t\"\"\"\n    14\timport concurrent.futures\n    15\timport hashlib\n    16\timport json\n    17\timport re\n    18\timport sys\n    19\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    20\t\n    21\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    22\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    23\timport census_readers  # noqa: E402\n    24\t\n    25\tFAMILIES = census_readers.FAMILIES\n    26\tD505 = '营收、毛利率的指引、市场预期、实际业绩 (revenue, gross-margin guidance, market expectations, reported results)'\n    27\t\n    28\tMERGE = \"\"\"# Blind reader: merge your own matter lists into one (read-only)\n    29\t\n    30\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst covering NVIDIA. Below are the questions and matters you listed, batch by batch, from {count} news\n    31\tarticles of 2026-09-07 to 09-20 (article numbers are global). Merge them into one list across all batches:\n    32\t- one matter = one underlying bet, whichever articles raise it;\n    33\t- another year of a figure is another matter;\n    34\t- two entries are one matter only when the same outcome would settle both and their answers cannot move apart.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib,collections\nd=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic\\\")\nr=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run\\\")\ncases={n:{x[\\\"thesis\\\"][\\\"id\\\"]:x[\\\"thesis\\\"][\\\"opens_as\\\"] for x in json.loads((r/n/\\\"cases/cases.json\\\").read_text())} for n in (\\\"new-2\\\",\\\"new-3\\\")}\nfor l,n in zip((3,5,6,1,2),(\\\"new-2\\\",\\\"new-3\\\",\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"old-2\\\",\\\"old-3\\\")):\n qs=json.loads((d/f\\\"mapping-out/library-L{l}.json\\\").read_text())\n print(n,collections.Counter(q[\\\"type\\\"] for q in qs),list(qs[0]))\n if n in cases:\n  print(\\\"cases\\\",collections.Counter(cases[n].values()))\n  print([(q[\\\"question_id\\\"],q[\\\"source_id\\\"]) for q in qs[:7]])\n allc=list((d/\\\"deck\\\").glob(\\\"C*.txt\\\"))\n whichqs={\\\"new-2\\\":[\\\"Q1\\\",\\\"Q32\\\"],\\\"new-3\\\":[\\\"Q1\\\",\\\"Q13\\\",\\\"Q20\\\",\\\"Q39\\\",\\\"Q38\\\"],\\\"old-1\\\":[\\\"Q8\\\",\\\"Q34\\\"],\\\"old-2\\\":[\\\"Q5\\\",\\\"Q9\\\",\\\"Q33\\\",\\\"Q40\\\"],\\\"old-3\\\":[\\\"Q6\\\",\\\"Q35\\\",\\\"Q47\\\"]}[n]\n for q in [q for q in qs if q[\\\"question_id\\\"] in whichqs]:\n  keyword=q[\\\"question\\\"]\n  print(q[\\\"question_id\\\"],q[\\\"opened\\\"],q[\\\"source_id\\\"],str(q[\\\"answer\\\"])[:180],[(c.stem) for c in allc if keyword in c.read_text().split(\\\"\\\\n\\\")[:8] or \\\"QUESTION: \\\"+keyword in c.read_text().split(\\\"\\\\n\\\")[:8]])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
