ed in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:9479:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:9735:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:9971:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:10317:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:10705:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:10911:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:11327:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:11597:  \"question\": \"Will the DOJ Antitrust Division or FTC take a publicly documented formal antitrust step concerning NVIDIA's revenue-sharing, credit-support or customer-financing arrangements by NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end on January 31, 2027?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:11904:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:12130:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:12378:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:12594:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:12742:  \"question\": \"Will the US Department of Justice publish a decision or conclusion on its antitrust review of NVIDIA's $20 billion Groq licensing/acqui-hire arrangement by January 31, 2027, as established by DOJ public statements, court filings, a consent decree, confirmation of closure, or NVIDIA's periodic filings?\",\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L5.json:12969:     \"question\": \"What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib,collections\nd=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic\\\")\nr=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run\\\")\nwanted={3:[1,32],5:[1,13,20,38,39],6:[8,34],1:[5,9,33,40],2:[6,35,47]}\ncards={tuple(c.read_text().splitlines()[:3]):c.stem for c in (d/\\\"deck\\\").glob(\\\"C*.txt\\\")}\nfor l,lqs in wanted.items():\n p=d/f\\\"mapping-out/library-L{l}.json\\\"\n qs=json.loads(p.read_text())\n for i in lqs:\n  q=qs[i-1]\n  print(l,i,*[c.stem for c in (d/\\\"deck\\\").glob(\\\"C*.txt\\\") if c.open().readline().strip()==\\\"Question: \\\"+q[\\\"question\\\"] and f\\\"Latest answer at the cutoff: {q['\\\\''answer'\\\\'']}\\\" in c.read_text()])\n  if l in (3,5) and i in (32,39):\n   print(q[\\\"possible_answers\\\"])\n   print(q[\\\"reasons\\\"])\nfor n in (\\\"new-2\\\",\\\"new-3\\\"):\n p=r/n/\\\"judge-inputs.json\\\"\n o=json.loads(p.read_text())\n print(n,type(o),str(o)[:1800])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib,collections\nd=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic\\\")\nr=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run\\\")\nwanted={3:[1,32],5:[1,13,20,38,39],6:[8,34],1:[5,9,33,40],2:[6,35,47]}\ncards={tuple(c.read_text().splitlines()[:3]):c.stem for c in (d/\\\"deck\\\").glob(\\\"C*.txt\\\")}\nfor l,lqs in wanted.items():\n p=d/f\\\"mapping-out/library-L{l}.json\\\"\n qs=json.loads(p.read_text())\n for i in lqs:\n  q=qs[i-1]\n  print(l,i,*[c.stem for c in (d/\\\"deck\\\").glob(\\\"C*.txt\\\") if c.open().readline().strip()==\\\"Question: \\\"+q[\\\"question\\\"] and f\\\"Latest answer at the cutoff: {q['\\\\''answer'\\\\'']}\\\" in c.read_text()])\n  if l in (3,5) and i in (32,39):\n   print(q[\\\"possible_answers\\\"])\n   print(q[\\\"reasons\\\"])\nfor n in (\\\"new-2\\\",\\\"new-3\\\"):\n p=r/n/\\\"judge-inputs.json\\\"\n o=json.loads(p.read_text())\n print(n,type(o),str(o)[:1800])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"3 1 C110\n3 32 C055\n['By January 31, 2027, the DOJ Antitrust Division has publicly announced its conclusion on the Groq transaction through a closing statement, an enforcement complaint, a settlement or a consent-decree filing; a published conclusion counts whether or not it entails enforcement.', \"After September 10, 2026, the DOJ Antitrust Division formally abandons or withdraws its review of Nvidia's Groq transaction without publishing a substantive decision or conclusion. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-10 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\", 'Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.']\nThe reference is rung 3 (Still open): no market consensus or DOJ deadline for a public resolution is supplied. The investigation and formal information request make a resolution by the deadline plausible, but they provide no timetable; the evidence therefore still makes the review remaining open on January 31, 2027, more likely than a public conclusion by then. No evidence establishes a formal abandonment after September 10, 2026, but its absence is not itself evidence that rung 2 holds.\n5 1 C154\n5 13\n5 20 C015\n5 38 C023\n5 39 C196\n['By January 31, 2027, the DOJ has publicly issued a decision or conclusion on the Groq arrangement, including an enforcement action or challenge, a consent decree or negotiated conditions, or confirmation of closure without enforcement, as documented in the specified materials.', 'After September 10, 2026, the US Department of Justice formally abandons or withdraws the review without publishing a decision or conclusion on the arrangement. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-10 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not.', 'Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.']\nThe available reference is a reported active DOJ review with no public resolution timetable, which sits at rung 3, Still open. The reported formal information request and possible negotiated conditions make a public conclusion possible, but neither supplies a concrete decision step or timing that makes a conclusion by January 31, 2027 more likely than the review remaining open. Rung 2 is unsupported: there is no evidence of a formal abandonment or withdrawal after September 10, 2026.\n6 8 C108\n6 34 C186\n1 5 C148\n1 9 C150\n1 33 C091\n1 40 C101\n2 6 C124 C016\n2 35 C074\n2 47 C099\nnew-2 <class 'dict'> {'new-2': {'trace': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/step1/trace.sqlite', 'step2': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/step2', 'ladders': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl', 'ladder_archive': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/ladder/send-1/archive.sqlite', 'cases': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/cases/cases.json', 'output_root': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/judge', 'end_day': '2026-09-20'}}\nnew-3 <class 'dict'> {'new-3': {'trace': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/step1/trace.sqlite', 'step2': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/step2', 'ladders': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl', 'ladder_archive': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/archive.sqlite', 'cases': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/cases/cases.json', 'output_root': 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/judge', 'end_day': '2026-09-20'}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for n in 148 074 150 091 101 099 023 055 196 110 154; do f=poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/deck/C$n.txt; nl -ba $f | sed -n '\"'1,21p'; done\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for n in 148 074 150 091 101 099 023 055 196 110 154; do f=poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/deck/C$n.txt; nl -ba $f | sed -n '\"'1,21p'; done\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tQuestion: How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-07-04\n     3\tDue: 2027-03-31\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. Combined spending is less than $500 billion.\n     6\t2. Combined spending is at least $500 billion and less than $600 billion.\n     7\t3. Combined spending is at least $600 billion and less than $700 billion.\n     8\t4. Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.\n     9\t5. Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n    10\t\n    11\tLatest answer at the cutoff: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.\n    12\tReason given: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four companies, which is rung 4. A direct four-hyperscaler estimate of $760 billion and tracking near $745 billion support an implied outcome around $750–$760 billion. Newer estimates of nearly $800 billion refer to the top five, not these four, so the fifth company cannot be counted in full; the four-company outcome remains below the $800 billion rung-5 boundary. The revised company guidance and Microsoft's reported capex growth support a high total, while the cancellation, grid-connection and sustainability concerns are not quantified as changes to the four-company 2026 total.\n    13\t\n    14\tMaterial that answer was given (42 items, by day):\n    15\t1. (fact; publisher 247wallst.com; speaker 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft; published 2026-07-22) Microsoft capital expenditures were $30.88 billion in Q3 FY2026, up 84% year over year.\n    16\t   Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n    17\t2. (party_statement; publisher fool.com; speaker fool.com (analyst); original source Alphabet 2026-07-22; published 2026-07-27) Alphabet raised its 2026 capital expenditure guidance to $195 billion–$205 billion, from $180 billion–$190 billion previously.\n    18\t   Quote: Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\n    19\t3. (party_statement; publisher fool.com; speaker fool.com (analyst); original source Sundar Pichai; published 2026-07-27) Alphabet CEO Sundar Pichai said demand for Alphabet's AI models was outpacing supply and that management was ramping up capital spending to convert backlog into revenue.\n    20\t   Quote: CEO Sundar Pichai noted that demand for its AI models is outpacing supply, prompting management to ramp up capex to quickly convert its massive backlog into revenue.\n    21\t4. (party_statement; publisher 247wallst.com; speaker 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source Meta Platforms; published 2026-08-01) Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n     1\tQuestion: What will NVIDIA report as data-center revenue for FY2027?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-08-19\n     3\tDue: 2027-02-28\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. FY2027 data-center revenue is at least $400 billion.\n     6\t2. FY2027 data-center revenue is at least $361 billion but less than $400 billion.\n     7\t3. FY2027 data-center revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $361 billion.\n     8\t4. FY2027 data-center revenue is at least $250 billion but less than $300 billion.\n     9\t5. FY2027 data-center revenue is less than $250 billion.\n    10\t\n    11\tLatest answer at the cutoff: FY2027 data-center revenue is at least $361 billion but less than $400 billion.\n    12\tReason given: The only supplied quantified market reference is Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits exactly at rung 2's floor. The implied outcome is about $374–375 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half; the Q3 outlook of about $108 billion in total revenue, applied to the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $100 billion of Data Center revenue; and roughly $110 billion in Q4 is consistent with management's expected reacceleration and the supplied forecast of more than $100 billion. That is above rung 2's $361 billion floor but below rung 1's $400 billion threshold; the new demand and supply evidence does not quantify enough additional FY2027 revenue to bridge the gap.\n    13\t\n    14\tMaterial that answer was given (69 items, by day):\n    15\t1. (third_party_view; publisher morningstar.com; speaker Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; published 2026-08-19) Morningstar estimates NVIDIA data-center revenue of $361 billion in FY2027, up 86% annually; it cites $194 billion in FY2026 and $3 billion in FY2020.\n    16\t   Quote: Nvidia’s DC business has achieved exponential growth already, rising from $3 billion in fiscal 2020 to $194 billion in fiscal 2026, and we estimate it will be $361 billion in fiscal 2027, representing 86% annual growth.\n    17\t2. (fact; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-22) NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75 billion, up 92% year over year.\n    18\t   Quote: In NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA)'s Q1 of fiscal year 2027, the firm's data center revenue jumped by 92% annually to sit at $75 billion.\n    19\t3. (fact; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-25) NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year.\n    20\t   Quote: NVIDIA's Q1 FY27 earnings were dominated by Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year, with networking growing 199%.\n    21\t4. (party_statement; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source Anthropic; published 2026-08-25) Anthropic planned to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of AMD Instinct MI450 GPUs in Helios beginning in the first half of 2027, in a deal potentially worth tens of billions of dollars over time.\n     1\tQuestion: How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-07-06\n     3\tDue: 2027-02-28\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion.\n     6\t2. FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion.\n     7\t3. FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion.\n     8\t4. FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion.\n     9\t5. FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\n    10\t\n    11\tLatest answer at the cutoff: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion.\n    12\tReason given: The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is now around $410 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half, Q3 total-revenue guidance and the recent Data Center mix imply roughly $100 billion for Q3, and a roughly $146 billion Q4 is supported by the expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp. SemiAnalysis's forecast of second-half Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates is a meaningful upside input: applying it to the roughly $250 billion of second-half revenue implied by the $414 billion reference would point to about $464 billion for the year. I discount that forecast because it does not state its underlying estimate and its implied Q4 is much higher than the company has quantified; the resulting central estimate remains near $410 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. Supply and competition risks, and NVIDIA's exclusion of China compute revenue from its outlook, temper the upside.\n    13\t\n    14\tMaterial that answer was given (123 items, by day):\n    15\t1. (fact; publisher tradingview.com; speaker tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-07-06) NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\n    16\t   Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n    17\t2. (third_party_view; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet; published 2026-07-07) Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA’s AI computing products.\n    18\t   Quote: The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors.\n    19\t3. (third_party_view; publisher mlq.ai; speaker mlq.ai; original source Industry surveys; published 2026-07-07) Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, versus roughly 30% currently, potentially reducing demand for NVIDIA accelerators in China.\n    20\t   Quote: Chinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5].\n    21\t4. (third_party_view; publisher fortune.com; speaker Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; published 2026-07-08) Chinese company executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\n     1\tQuestion: What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-08-26\n     3\tDue: 2028-02-28\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. FY2028 gross margin is at least 75%.\n     6\t2. FY2028 gross margin is at least 73% and below 75%.\n     7\t3. FY2028 gross margin is at least 71% and below 73%.\n     8\t4. FY2028 gross margin is at least 69% and below 71%.\n     9\t5. FY2028 gross margin is below 69%.\n    10\t\n    11\tLatest answer at the cutoff: FY2028 gross margin is at least 71% and below 73%.\n    12\tReason given: The reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2028 gross-margin guidance of 72%–73%, which sits at rung 3. The implied outcome remains about 72.5%, the midpoint of that direct FY2028 guidance. The guided path—75% in FY2027 Q2, 74% in Q3, and 71%–72% in Q4 before recovery—supports the forecast, while worsening memory-cost and supply signals add downside risk without quantifying a further FY2028 reduction. The implied figure remains within 71%–below 73%, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\n    13\t\n    14\tMaterial that answer was given (31 items, by day):\n    15\t1. (party_statement; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker Colette Kress, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said rising component costs and extreme memory pricing conditions prompted the company to reset its gross-margin expectations.\n    16\t   Quote: Many of you have expressed concerns regarding our gross margins as component costs have risen significantly. As you are already aware, we are experiencing extreme pricing conditions in memory... As a result, we are resetting expectations today,\n    17\t2. (party_statement; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker Colette Kress, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margin was expected to bottom at 71–72% in FY2027 Q4 and settle at 72–73% in FY2028 as price increases take effect in Q1.\n    18\t   Quote: We expect margins to bottom in Q4 in the 71% to 72% range before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal year '28, as executed price increases take effect in Q1,\n    19\t3. (party_statement; publisher foxbusiness.com; speaker Jensen Huang, in an interview with Fox Business; original source Jensen Huang; published 2026-08-27) Huang said NVIDIA had repriced its products in the marketplace; he gave no price change or timing.\n    20\t   Quote: We have also repriced our products in the marketplace\n    21\t4. (fact; publisher insidermonkey.com; speaker Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-27) NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of 75.0%.\n     1\tQuestion: Will NVIDIA invest $10 billion to anchor Anthropic's IPO by 2026-09-20?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-09-14\n     3\tDue: 2026-09-20\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. NVIDIA completes an investment of $10 billion to anchor Anthropic's IPO by 2026-09-20.\n     6\t2. NVIDIA does not complete an investment of $10 billion to anchor Anthropic's IPO by 2026-09-20.\n     7\t\n     8\tLatest answer at the cutoff: NVIDIA does not complete an investment of $10 billion to anchor Anthropic's IPO by 2026-09-20.\n     9\tReason given: The reference is rung 2: no market consensus or company guidance is supplied, and the reported expectation that Anthropic's IPO would be completed before the November midterms does not put NVIDIA's investment by September 20. Reuters-linked reports that NVIDIA is considering up to $10 billion and that Anthropic is discussing an anchor role push toward Yes, but they describe consideration and discussion, not a commitment or a completed investment. With only three days to the deadline, the most likely outcome remains that NVIDIA does not complete a $10 billion anchor investment by then. The reported IPO scale and Anthropic's growth make the proposed transaction plausible, but do not establish that it will close within that window; no rung boundary is crossed.\n    10\t\n    11\tMaterial that answer was given (9 items, by day):\n    12\t1. (rumour; publisher fxleaders.com; speaker fxleaders.com, citing Reuters; original source Reuters; published 2026-09-14) Reuters reported that NVIDIA was considering anchoring Anthropic's IPO with a $10 billion investment during the week of September 14, 2026.\n    13\t   Quote: NVIDIA is reportedly considering anchoring a $10 billion Anthropic IPO this week, according to Reuters.\n    14\t2. (third_party_view; publisher fxleaders.com; speaker fxleaders.com; original source fxleaders.com 2026-09-14; published 2026-09-14) The reported potential Anthropic offering could sell up to $100 billion in shares at an approximately $2 trillion valuation.\n    15\t   Quote: The offering could sell up to $100 billion worth of shares at a valuation around $2 trillion\n    16\t3. (rumour; publisher insidermonkey.com; speaker InsiderMonkey; original source Reuters 2026-09-13; published 2026-09-15) Reuters reported on September 13, 2026, that NVIDIA was considering investing up to $10 billion in Anthropic as an anchor investor in a potential IPO.\n    17\t   Quote: Nvidia is reportedly considering an investment of up to $10 billion.\n    18\t4. (rumour; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com; published 2026-09-15) NVIDIA is reportedly in advanced talks to invest up to US$10 billion in Anthropic’s planned IPO.\n    19\t   Quote: Nvidia (NasdaqGS: NVDA) is in advanced talks to invest up to US$10b in Anthropic's planned IPO, according to people familiar with the discussions.\n    20\t5. (rumour; publisher barchart.com; speaker barchart.com; original source Reuters; published 2026-09-17) Reuters reported that Anthropic was discussing making Nvidia an anchor investor in its planned IPO, with Nvidia reportedly considering an investment of up to $10 billion.\n    21\t   Quote: Anthropic is discussing bringing Nvidia in as an anchor investor for its planned IPO, according to Reuters. Nvidia is reportedly considering an investment of up to $10 billion, although neither company has confirmed the discussions.\n     1\tQuestion: Will NVIDIA agree to anchor Anthropic’s IPO with an investment of $10 billion by September 20, 2026?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-09-14\n     3\tDue: 2026-09-20\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. NVIDIA agrees by September 20, 2026, to anchor Anthropic’s IPO with a $10 billion investment.\n     6\t2. NVIDIA has not agreed by September 20, 2026, to anchor Anthropic’s IPO with a $10 billion investment.\n     7\t\n     8\tLatest answer at the cutoff: NVIDIA has not agreed by September 20, 2026, to anchor Anthropic’s IPO with a $10 billion investment.\n     9\tReason given: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or NVIDIA commitment is supplied. The reports of advanced talks and consideration of an investment of up to $10 billion this week raise the chance of Yes, but they do not report agreement to anchor the IPO for $10 billion. Given the short time to the deadline and the gap between considering an investment and agreeing to the exact commitment in the question, No remains the more likely outcome.\n    10\t\n    11\tMaterial that answer was given (5 items, by day):\n    12\t1. (rumour; publisher fxleaders.com; speaker fxleaders.com; original source Reuters; published 2026-09-14) NVIDIA is reportedly considering anchoring Anthropic’s IPO with a $10 billion investment this week.\n    13\t   Quote: NVIDIA is reportedly considering anchoring a $10 billion Anthropic IPO this week, according to Reuters.\n    14\t2. (rumour; publisher insidermonkey.com; speaker Insider Monkey; original source Reuters 2026-09-13; published 2026-09-15) Reuters reported on September 13 that NVIDIA was considering investing up to $10 billion as an anchor investor in Anthropic’s planned IPO, which could raise up to $100 billion at an approximately $2 trillion valuation.\n    15\t   Quote: On September 13, Reuters reported that Anthropic is in talks to bring NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) in as an anchor investor in what could become one of the largest IPOs in history, potentially raising as much as $100 billion at a valuation of around $2 trillion. Nvidia is reportedly considering an investment of up to $10 billion.\n    16\t3. (rumour; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com, reporting unnamed sources; published 2026-09-15) Unnamed sources said NVIDIA was in advanced talks to invest up to US$10 billion in Anthropic’s planned IPO.\n    17\t   Quote: Nvidia (NasdaqGS: NVDA) is in advanced talks to invest up to US$10b in Anthropic's planned IPO, according to people familiar with the discussions.\n    18\t4. (rumour; publisher barchart.com; speaker barchart.com; original source Reuters, reporting confidential discussions; published 2026-09-17) Anthropic is reportedly discussing NVIDIA as an anchor investor in its planned IPO, with NVIDIA considering an investment of up to $10 billion.\n    19\t   Quote: Anthropic is discussing bringing Nvidia in as an anchor investor for its planned IPO, according to Reuters. Nvidia is reportedly considering an investment of up to $10 billion, although neither company has confirmed the discussions.\n    20\t5. (rumour; publisher barchart.com; speaker barchart.com; original source Reuters; published 2026-09-17) Anthropic's proposed IPO valuation is about $2 trillion, versus a $965 billion post-money valuation after its May 2026 $65 billion fundraising.\n    21\t   Quote: A $2 trillion valuation would represent a substantial increase from Anthropic's $965 billion post-money valuation in May, when it raised $65 billion.\n     1\tQuestion: Will the FOMC raise its federal funds target range by at least 0.25 percentage points at its September 15–16, 2026 meeting, as established by its September 16 policy statement and target-range announcement?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-09-06\n     3\tDue: 2026-09-16\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. The FOMC announces an increase of at least 0.25 percentage points in its federal funds target range at the September 15–16, 2026 meeting, by September 16, 2026.\n     6\t2. After September 6, 2026, the FOMC formally decides at its September 15–16 meeting to leave the federal funds target range unchanged, lower it, or raise it by less than 0.25 percentage points. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-06 and stands on 2026-09-16, and only when rung 1 does not.\n     7\t3. Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2026-09-16.\n     8\t\n     9\tLatest answer at the cutoff: The FOMC announces an increase of at least 0.25 percentage points in its federal funds target range at the September 15–16, 2026 meeting, by September 16, 2026.\n    10\tReason given: The market reference is an approximately 86% probability of a September rate increase, which places the expected outcome on rung 1. That is a stronger hike signal than the roughly 60% probability previously reported; the July quarter-point dissents and the reported inflation and jobs context also support a hike. The evidence does not establish that the FOMC has already decided, but as a forecast a hike of at least 0.25 percentage points is the most likely outcome, so rung 1 remains the best fit.\n    11\t\n    12\tMaterial that answer was given (2 items, by day):\n    13\t1. (third_party_view; publisher finance.biggo.com; speaker finance.biggo.com; original source Interest-rate futures market (as reported) and FOMC participants; published 2026-09-06) Markets were pricing in roughly a 60% probability of a Federal Reserve rate hike at the September 15–16, 2026 meeting, the item reports, with three-month annualized core PCE running just over 3% and three Fed officials (Beth Hammack, Neel Kashkari, Lorie Logan) having dissented in favour of a quarter-point hike at the July meeting.\n    14\t   Quote: Markets are pricing in roughly 60% probability of a rate hike at the September meeting, bolstered by a strong jobs report on Friday. … The three-month annualized core PCE rate -- the Fed's preferred gauge -- is running at just over 3%. … Three Fed officials dissented at the July meeting in favor of a quarter-point hike: Beth Hammack, Neel Kashkari and Lorie Logan.\n    15\t2. (third_party_view; publisher qz.com; speaker qz.com; original source Fed funds futures markets 2026-09-14; published 2026-09-14) Fed funds futures markets assigned roughly an 86% probability to a Federal Reserve rate increase at the September 2026 policy meeting, which the article says opens this week, as reported by qz.com.\n    16\t   Quote: The Federal Reserve opens its September policy meeting this week, with fed funds futures markets assigning about an 86% chance to a rate increase.\n    17\t\n    18\tPrevious answer, given for comparison: 1. The FOMC announces an increase of at least 0.25 percentage points in its federal funds target range at the September 15–16, 2026 meeting, by September 16, 2026.; reason: The reference is the futures market’s roughly 60% probability of a September hike, which places the market expectation on rung 1. The reported strong jobs data, core PCE running just over 3%, and three officials’ preference for a quarter-point hike at the July meeting reinforce a hike as the most likely September outcome. The evidence does not establish that the FOMC has already decided; this is a forecast, and the likely hike crosses the rung 1 boundary.\n    19\t\n    20\tThe library's questions in opening order (this card's question marked):\n    21\t- opened 2026-07-01: What aggregate amount in USD of guarantees, credit support and other contingent backstop obligations to AI cloud partners under NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing AI-factory model is disclosed as outstanding at FY2027 year-end in its FY2027 Form 10-K, supplemented by its Form 10-Q disclosures, counting an explicit confirmation of no material obligations as zero for this disclosed-material-amount measure?\n     1\tQuestion: Will the DOJ Antitrust Division publish a formal resolution of its review of Nvidia's Groq licensing transaction by January 31, 2027, as evidenced by a public DOJ statement or court filing?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-09-10\n     3\tDue: 2027-01-31\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. By January 31, 2027, the DOJ Antitrust Division has publicly announced its conclusion on the Groq transaction through a closing statement, an enforcement complaint, a settlement or a consent-decree filing; a published conclusion counts whether or not it entails enforcement.\n     6\t2. After September 10, 2026, the DOJ Antitrust Division formally abandons or withdraws its review of Nvidia's Groq transaction without publishing a substantive decision or conclusion. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-10 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\n     7\t3. Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n     8\t\n     9\tLatest answer at the cutoff: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n    10\tReason given: The reference is rung 3 (Still open): no market consensus or DOJ deadline for a public resolution is supplied. The investigation and formal information request make a resolution by the deadline plausible, but they provide no timetable; the evidence therefore still makes the review remaining open on January 31, 2027, more likely than a public conclusion by then. No evidence establishes a formal abandonment after September 10, 2026, but its absence is not itself evidence that rung 2 holds.\n    11\t\n    12\tMaterial that answer was given (9 items, by day):\n    13\t1. (rumour; publisher gurufocus.com; speaker Bloomberg, citing people familiar with the matter; reported by GuruFocus; original source Bloomberg; published 2026-09-10) The U.S. Department of Justice is examining whether Nvidia's $20 billion arrangement with AI-chip startup Groq — unveiled in December, giving Nvidia access to Groq's technology while allowing Groq to keep operating separately — was designed to limit antitrust scrutiny, Bloomberg reported citing people familiar with the matter. The Justice Department began examining the transaction earlier this year as part of a broader antitrust review, and the investigation could conclude without any enforcement action. Groq CEO Jonathan Ross and COO Sunny Madra subsequently moved to Nvidia, and U.S. lawmakers have questioned whether the arrangement amounts to an acquisition that could reduce competition in the AI-chip industry.\n    14\t   Quote: Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) shares dipped about 1% early Thursday as a U.S. Justice Department review examined whether its $20 billion arrangement with AI chip startup Groq was designed to limit antitrust scrutiny, Bloomberg reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The inquiry focuses on a transaction unveiled in December that gave Nvidia access to Groq's technology while allowing the startu\n    15\t2. (party_statement; publisher gurufocus.com; speaker GuruFocus; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-09-10) Nvidia defended its Groq arrangement, stating that the agreement allows it to provide Groq technology across its ecosystem while leaving Groq's cloud operation independently funded and operating.\n    16\t   Quote: Nvidia has defended the transaction, saying the agreement allows it to provide Groq technology across its ecosystem while leaving Groq's cloud operation independently funded and operating.\n    17\t3. (rumour; publisher barchart.com; speaker Barchart (citing unnamed reports); original source U.S. Department of Justice; published 2026-09-10) The U.S. Department of Justice is reportedly intensifying its antitrust scrutiny of Nvidia's $17 billion licensing deal with AI startup Groq, investigating whether Nvidia structured the agreement to avoid antitrust scrutiny; the DOJ opened its inquiry shortly after the deal was announced and has since issued Nvidia a formal request for information, according to the reports cited. The arrangement, announced last year as a non-exclusive technology licensing deal, also brought Groq chief executive Jonathan Ross and other senior executives to Nvidia while Groq remained independent.\n    18\t   Quote: Nvidia (NVDA) stock inched lower on Sept. 10 following reports that the U.S. Department of Justice (DOJ) is intensifying its regulatory scrutiny of the titan's $17 billion deal with artificial intelligence (AI) startup Groq. … The Department of Justice is investigating whether Nvidia structured its agreement with Groq to avoid antitrust scrutiny. … Announced last year as a non-exclusive technology\n    19\t4. (rumour; publisher barchart.com; speaker Barchart; original source Unnamed reports cited by Barchart; published 2026-09-10) According to the reports cited, the U.S. Department of Justice is unlikely to force Nvidia's Groq licensing deal to be unwound, a view the article uses to argue the recent share-price pullback is a buying opportunity.\n    20\t   Quote: The recent pullback in Nvidia stock may actually be a compelling buying opportunity for long-term investors since the DOJ is reportedly unlikely to force the deal to be unwound.\n    21\t5. (fact; publisher sdxcentral.com; speaker The New York Times, as reported by SDxCentral (Ben Wodecki); original source U.S. Department of Justice; published 2026-09-10) The New York Times reported that U.S. Department of Justice antitrust officials have sent Nvidia a formal request for information about its Groq deal — the 'nonexclusive licensing agreement' disclosed in late December under which Groq founder and then-CEO Jonathan Ross and key Groq staff joined Nvidia — with an investigation commenced after that disclosure and antitrust officials reportedly examining whether Nvidia sought to skirt an antitrust probe.\n     1\tQuestion: Will the US Department of Justice publish a decision or conclusion on its antitrust review of NVIDIA's $20 billion Groq licensing/acqui-hire arrangement by January 31, 2027, as established by DOJ public statements, court filings, a consent decree, confirmation of closure, or NVIDIA's periodic filings?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-09-10\n     3\tDue: 2027-01-31\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. By January 31, 2027, the DOJ has publicly issued a decision or conclusion on the Groq arrangement, including an enforcement action or challenge, a consent decree or negotiated conditions, or confirmation of closure without enforcement, as documented in the specified materials.\n     6\t2. After September 10, 2026, the US Department of Justice formally abandons or withdraws the review without publishing a decision or conclusion on the arrangement. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-10 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\n     7\t3. Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n     8\t\n     9\tLatest answer at the cutoff: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n    10\tReason given: The available reference is a reported active DOJ review with no public resolution timetable, which sits at rung 3, Still open. The reported formal information request and possible negotiated conditions make a public conclusion possible, but neither supplies a concrete decision step or timing that makes a conclusion by January 31, 2027 more likely than the review remaining open. Rung 2 is unsupported: there is no evidence of a formal abandonment or withdrawal after September 10, 2026.\n    11\t\n    12\tMaterial that answer was given (13 items, by day):\n    13\t1. (fact; publisher techcrunch.com; speaker TechCrunch; original source Groq; published 2026-08-17) Groq shifted from being a pure AI chipmaker building its own LPUs to compete with Nvidia on inference into a cloud and data-centre provider that operates Nvidia systems, after Nvidia hired founder and CEO Jonathan Ross and other top talent in a $20 billion licensing deal last September; the pivot was kicked off with a $650 million round in June.\n    14\t   Quote: Groq was focused on building its own chips, dubbed LPUs (language processing units), to compete with Nvidia on inference — the type of compute needed to run AI workloads in real time. After it lost its star team, Groq shifted from being a pure AI chipmaker into a cloud and data center provider that operates Nvidia systems. … In June, Groq raised a $650 million round to kick off its pivot.\n    15\t2. (rumour; publisher gurufocus.com; speaker Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg 2026-09-10; published 2026-09-10) Bloomberg, citing people familiar with the matter, reported that the U.S. Justice Department is reviewing whether Nvidia's $20 billion arrangement with AI chip startup Groq was designed to limit antitrust scrutiny. The review focuses on a transaction unveiled in December that gave Nvidia access to Groq's technology while allowing Groq to continue operating separately, with Groq CEO Jonathan Ross and COO Sunny Madra subsequently moving to Nvidia. The Justice Department began examining the transaction earlier this year as part of a broader antitrust review, and the report said the investigation could conclude without enforcement action.\n    16\t   Quote: Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) shares dipped about 1% early Thursday as a U.S. Justice Department review examined whether its $20 billion arrangement with AI chip startup Groq was designed to limit antitrust scrutiny, Bloomberg reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The inquiry focuses on a transaction unveiled in December that gave Nvidia access to Groq's technology while allowing the startu\n    17\t3. (rumour; publisher barchart.com; speaker barchart.com; original source barchart.com; published 2026-09-10) The US Department of Justice is intensifying its regulatory scrutiny of Nvidia's $17 billion deal with AI startup Groq, investigating whether Nvidia structured the agreement to avoid antitrust scrutiny; the DOJ reportedly opened its inquiry shortly after the non-exclusive technology licensing deal was announced last year and has since issued Nvidia a formal request for information. The arrangement also brought Groq CEO Jonathan Ross and other senior executives to Nvidia while Groq remained independent.\n    18\t   Quote: Nvidia (NVDA) stock inched lower on Sept. 10 following reports that the U.S. Department of Justice (DOJ) is intensifying its regulatory scrutiny of the titan's $17 billion deal with artificial intelligence (AI) startup Groq. … The Department of Justice is investigating whether Nvidia structured its agreement with Groq to avoid antitrust scrutiny. … Announced last year as a non-exclusive technology\n    19\t4. (rumour; publisher barchart.com; speaker barchart.com; original source barchart.com; published 2026-09-10) Barchart.com reports that the DOJ is reportedly unlikely to force Nvidia's Groq deal to be unwound, presenting this as a reason the recent pullback is a buying opportunity. The claim is attributed only to reports, not to the DOJ or Nvidia.\n    20\t   Quote: The recent pullback in Nvidia stock may actually be a compelling buying opportunity for long-term investors since the DOJ is reportedly unlikely to force the deal to be unwound.\n    21\t5. (rumour; publisher sdxcentral.com; speaker Ben Wodecki, sdxcentral.com, citing The New York Times; original source The New York Times; published 2026-09-10) The New York Times reported that US Department of Justice officials sent Nvidia a formal request for information about its Groq licensing/acqui-hire deal — a deal the article says was disclosed in late December and reported to have cost about $20 billion — with antitrust officials reportedly examining whether the chip company sought to skirt an antitrust probe; sdxcentral, citing the Times, frames it as reports the DOJ could look into the deal.\n     1\tQuestion: What is the total USD amount of NVIDIA's outstanding guarantees and other credit-support commitments extended to AI cloud customers under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model at FY2027 year-end (January 31, 2027), without double-counting, as disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K and supporting commitment disclosures?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-07-01\n     3\tDue: 2027-04-01\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. below 1,000,000,000 USD\n     6\t2. from 1,000,000,000 USD to below 5,000,000,000 USD\n     7\t3. from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 10,000,000,000 USD\n     8\t4. from 10,000,000,000 USD to below 20,000,000,000 USD\n     9\t5. 20,000,000,000 USD or more\n    10\t\n    11\tLatest answer at the cutoff: 20,000,000,000 USD or more\n    12\tReason given: The reference is no market consensus, company guidance, or stated year-end target; the ladder's neutral reference is rung 3 ($5B to below $10B). The evidence implies an outstanding amount around $35B–$40B at January 31, 2027: start from NVIDIA's $36B of commitments under the new model as of July 26, allow for some utilization over the following six months, and account for the separately disclosed $3.5B maximum gross exposure on select AI-cloud-partner lease guarantees to the extent it is not already captured in that commitment figure. SharonAI's $4.88B backstop was announced before the July 26 baseline and is not added again; the Nscale guarantee is likewise not added on top of the $3.5B aggregate exposure. This estimate remains well above rung 5's $20B boundary. The $105B SB Energy/OpenAI guarantee is a separate project and is not treated as support extended to AI cloud customers under this model. Reports that some deals were paused temper the outlook, but NVIDIA said the broader model remains in place, and neither the pause nor subsequent disclosures support a reduction below the top rung.\n    13\t\n    14\tMaterial that answer was given (42 items, by day):\n    15\t1. (fact; publisher newsletter.semianalysis.com; speaker SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI; published 2026-07-06) SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop. SharonAI disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years. Its footprint is set to grow to 132MW (102MW contracted) and it guides to more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\n    16\t   Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company\n    17\t2. (party_statement; publisher newsletter.semianalysis.com; speaker SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; published 2026-07-06) Firmus's 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia — announced on June 29, 2026 and likely housed in a DayOne facility — is a major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop; Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, a portion of which (above the backstop level) it expects to share with NVIDIA.\n    18\t   Quote: Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program. … Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six\n    19\t3. (party_statement; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA said it will provide selective credit enhancement for nearly 2 gigawatts of compute for another frontier AI lab, complementing capacity that lab secured independently; it expects AI labs where it leverages its balance sheet to contribute roughly a quarter of its business next year and said shipped compute will be consumed by investment-grade customers or those backed by one, and that it is not making loans.\n    20\t   Quote: For another frontier AI lab, we will provide selective credit enhancement for nearly 2 gigawatts of compute. This complements the substantial NVIDIA compute capacity they've secured independently without NVIDIA's credit support. … For context, we expect demand from the AI labs for which we expect to leverage our balance sheet to contribute toward roughly a quarter of our business next year. This r\n    21\t4. (fact; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA disclosed maximum gross guarantee exposure of $108.5 billion, comprising $3.5 billion of land, power, and shell guarantees for certain AI cloud partners' data center lease obligations and $105 billion of guarantees supporting the SB Energy PORTS-Pike Technology Campus in Ohio. The SB Energy guarantees, entered in August 2026, support approximately 4.25 GW of buildout exclusively leased to OpenAI under 20-year leases, become effective in phases with the first expected in fiscal 2029, and decline as OpenAI fulfills lease payments; NVIDIA has an option to provide credit support for approximately 3.8 additional GW and says each generation at the site could represent approximately 1.5 million NVIDIA GPUs or $150 billion to $200 billion in NVIDIA revenue.\n     1\tQuestion: What aggregate amount in USD of guarantees, credit support and other contingent backstop obligations to AI cloud partners under NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing AI-factory model is disclosed as outstanding at FY2027 year-end in its FY2027 Form 10-K, supplemented by its Form 10-Q disclosures, counting an explicit confirmation of no material obligations as zero for this disclosed-material-amount measure?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-07-01\n     3\tDue: 2027-04-01\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. below 100,000,000 USD\n     6\t2. from 100,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000 USD\n     7\t3. from 1,000,000,000 USD to below 5,000,000,000 USD\n     8\t4. from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 10,000,000,000 USD\n     9\t5. 10,000,000,000 USD or more\n    10\t\n    11\tLatest answer at the cutoff: 10,000,000,000 USD or more\n    12\tReason given: The reference is the ladder's no-reference baseline: rung 3 ($1 billion to below $5 billion). The implied amount remains well above rung 5's $10 billion boundary: the $36 billion of commitments disclosed as of July 26, 2026, typically spanning six years, implies about $32.9 billion outstanding by January 31, 2027 under a simple pro-rata six-month run-off proxy. The reported pause and subsequent step-away concern new deal activity, not a quantified cancellation of existing commitments. The separate lease and SB Energy guarantees, and the Nscale lease guarantee, are not established as obligations under this revenue-sharing model and are not added.\n    13\t\n    14\tMaterial that answer was given (25 items, by day):\n    15\t1. (fact; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker NVIDIA Corporation (company blog); original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; published 2026-07-01) NVIDIA announced it is introducing a new business model in which AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers under a revenue-sharing and credit-support model: the AI clouds sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services while NVIDIA earns both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, which the company describes as a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream. NVIDIA's lead statement frames the arrangement as partnering with AI clouds to deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories with economics aligned through revenue sharing and credit support. No size, partner-level amount or signing date for the credit support is given.\n    16\t   Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … To address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players. … This new model enables AI c\n    17\t2. (fact; publisher newsletter.semianalysis.com; speaker SemiAnalysis; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; published 2026-07-06) NVIDIA has begun backstopping GPU rental offtakes for Neoclouds: it provides a take-or-pay commitment, described as a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity, and in exchange shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level. SemiAnalysis also reports NVIDIA has started backstopping datacenter leases.\n    18\t   Quote: This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level. … Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as\n    19\t3. (third_party_view; publisher newsletter.semianalysis.com; speaker SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; published 2026-07-06) SemiAnalysis describes NVIDIA's backstop program as typically six years long, during which NVIDIA stands ready to buy compute at pre-agreed prices that vary over time; each Neocloud negotiates separately, so revenue-share and backstop schedules may differ. Using an illustrative backstop pricing curve at the lower end of the range, SemiAnalysis cites an average of $2.36 over the six years and expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\n    20\t   Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative\n    21\t4. (fact; publisher newsletter.semianalysis.com; speaker SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI; published 2026-07-06) SharonAI announced in June 2026 a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop. SharonAI disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, implying an average floor of about $2.33/hour/GPU over six years; its footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs total (including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s) by mid-2027.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,pathlib,json\nr=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run\\\")\ndef ro(path):return sqlite3.connect(path.absolute().as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nfor p, look in [(r/\\\"new-2/judge/draw-new-2/views.sqlite\\\",[\\\"views\\\",\\\"clean_ladders\\\"]),(r/\\\"new-2/step1/trace.sqlite\\\",[\\\"trace\\\"]),(r/\\\"new-1/step1-redo/trace.sqlite\\\",[\\\"trace\\\"])]:\n print(p)\n db=ro(p)\n print(list(db.execute(\\\"select name,sql from sqlite_master where type='\\\\''table'\\\\'' and name in ('\\\\''views'\\\\'','\\\\''clean_ladders'\\\\'','\\\\''trace'\\\\'','\\\\''steps'\\\\'','\\\\''run'\\\\'','\\\\''theses'\\\\'')\\\"))[:10])\n print(list(db.execute(\\\"select name from sqlite_master where type='\\\\''table'\\\\''\\\")))\nfor p in sorted(r.glob(\\\"*/run.json\\\")):\n o=json.loads(p.read_text())\n print(p.parent.name,o.get(\\\"state\\\"),[(c,x.get(\\\"state\\\"),x.get(\\\"folder\\\")) for c,x in o[\\\"components\\\"].items()])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,pathlib,json\nr=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run\\\")\ndef ro(path):return sqlite3.connect(path.absolute().as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nfor p, look in [(r/\\\"new-2/judge/draw-new-2/views.sqlite\\\",[\\\"views\\\",\\\"clean_ladders\\\"]),(r/\\\"new-2/step1/trace.sqlite\\\",[\\\"trace\\\"]),(r/\\\"new-1/step1-redo/trace.sqlite\\\",[\\\"trace\\\"])]:\n print(p)\n db=ro(p)\n print(list(db.execute(\\\"select name,sql from sqlite_master where type='\\\\''table'\\\\'' and name in ('\\\\''views'\\\\'','\\\\''clean_ladders'\\\\'','\\\\''trace'\\\\'','\\\\''steps'\\\\'','\\\\''run'\\\\'','\\\\''theses'\\\\'')\\\"))[:10])\n print(list(db.execute(\\\"select name from sqlite_master where type='\\\\''table'\\\\''\\\")))\nfor p in sorted(r.glob(\\\"*/run.json\\\")):\n o=json.loads(p.read_text())\n print(p.parent.name,o.get(\\\"state\\\"),[(c,x.get(\\\"state\\\"),x.get(\\\"folder\\\")) for c,x in o[\\\"components\\\"].items()])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/judge/draw-new-2/views.sqlite\n[('views', 'CREATE TABLE views (view_id text primary key, object_id text, object_kind text, kind text, looks_at text, question text, due_day text, year text, created_seq integer, created_from_input text, created_at text)'), ('clean_ladders', \"CREATE TABLE clean_ladders (thesis_id, period not null default '', view_id, status, reason, reference_status, checked_answer, outcome_record, config_checksum, article_seq, article_day, primary key (thesis_id, period))\")]\n[('views',), ('rungs',), ('view_keys',), ('view_switches',), ('sqlite_sequence',), ('judgements',), ('evidence',), ('evidence_sources',), ('evidence_links',), ('replacements',), ('revisions',), ('same_claims',), ('dependencies',), ('dependency_resolutions',), ('successions',), ('handovers',), ('match_records',), ('processing_steps',), ('review_pool_entries',), ('late_statements',), ('days_completed',), ('thesis_groups',), ('thesis_group_members',), ('thesis_group_aliases',), ('groups_fts',), ('groups_fts_data',), ('groups_fts_idx',), ('groups_fts_content',), ('groups_fts_docsize',), ('groups_fts_config',), ('views_fts',), ('views_fts_data',), ('views_fts_idx',), ('views_fts_content',), ('views_fts_docsize',), ('views_fts_config',), ('clean_ladders',)]\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/step1/trace.sqlite\n[('run', 'CREATE TABLE run (manifest text not null, settings text not null, source_items text not null, initial_theses text not null)'), ('trace', 'CREATE TABLE trace (sequence integer primary key, input_id text not null, before_state text not null,\\n            request text not null, raw_response text, result text, after_state text, status text not null, error text, reserved_usd real,\\n            retry_reason text)')]\n[('run',), ('trace',), ('model_calls',), ('sqlite_sequence',)]\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-1/step1-redo/trace.sqlite\n[('run', 'CREATE TABLE run (manifest text not null, settings text not null, source_items text not null, initial_theses text not null)'), ('trace', 'CREATE TABLE trace (sequence integer primary key, input_id text not null, before_state text not null,\\n            request text not null, raw_response text, result text, after_state text, status text not null, error text, reserved_usd real,\\n            retry_reason text)')]\n[('run',), ('trace',), ('model_calls',), ('sqlite_sequence',)]\nnew-1 stopped [('step1', 'completed', 'step1-patched'), ('step2', None, None)]\nnew-2 complete [('step1', 'completed', 'step1'), ('step2', 'completed', 'step2'), ('cases', 'completed', 'cases'), ('ladder', 'completed', 'ladder'), ('judge', 'completed', 'judge')]\nnew-3 complete [('step1', 'completed', 'step1'), ('step2', 'completed', 'step2'), ('cases', 'completed', 'cases'), ('ladder', 'completed', 'ladder'), ('judge', 'completed', 'judge')]\nold-1 complete [('prod', 'completed', 'prod')]\nold-2 complete [('prod', 'completed', 'prod')]\nold-3 complete [('prod', 'completed', 'prod')]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for n in 148 074 150 091 101 099 023 055 196 110 154; do echo C$n; find poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/deck/round-{0,1,2} -name \"C$n-*.json\" -exec sh -c '\"'for f; do head -c 320 \\\"\"'$f\"; done'\"' sh {} +; done\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for n in 148 074 150 091 101 099 023 055 196 110 154; do echo C$n; find poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/deck/round-{0,1,2} -name \"C$n-*.json\" -exec sh -c '\"'for f; do head -c 320 \\\"\"'$f\"; done'\"' sh {} +; done\"","aggregated_output":"C148\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"Hyperscaler spending is a major driver of NVIDIA’s demand, and no earlier question tracks the same outcome. Direct four-company estimates of $720–$760 billion support the selected range; estimates near $800 billion cover five companies.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"Big-four hyperscaler capex is NVIDIA's main demand driver; no earlier question covers it. Guidance (Amazon ~220, Alphabet 195–205, Meta 125–145, plus Microsoft) and four-company estimates of $720–760B fit rung 4; the ~$800B figures include Oracle.\"}\nC074\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"FY27 data-center revenue is NVIDIA's core bet, distinct from the total-revenue question. H1 $164B plus ~$100B Q3 and a reaccelerating Q4 (~$110B) gives ~$375B. Rung 1 needs a ~$136B Q4; rung 3 needs a sequential decline.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"Data-center revenue is material and can move independently of total revenue. Reported first-half sales, the Q3 outlook and expected Q4 growth support a roughly $374 billion outcome; the material does not quantify enough upside to favor $400 billion or more.\"}\nC150\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"unsupported\", \"why\": \"Data Center revenue is central to NVIDIA’s business and distinct from the earlier questions. The $146 billion Q4 estimate requires roughly 46% sequential growth; qualitative hyperscale reacceleration and the Rubin ramp do not justify that jump over the previous esti{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"unsupported\", \"why\": \"FY27 Data Center revenue is a core bet; no earlier listed question duplicates it. Q4 jumps from ~$122B to $146B (+46% sequentially, versus a ~12% Q3 guide) without new quantified evidence, despite supply-constraint items; rung 3 fits better.\"}\nC091\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"FY2028 gross margin materially affects profit and is distinct from FY2027 margin. Management’s 72%–73% guidance centers within the selected range; memory-cost risks do not establish a more likely neighboring range.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"FY2028 margin is a material profit bet, separate from FY2027. Repeated management guidance of 72–73% (midpoint 72.5%) sits in rung 3. Memory-cost risk points lower, not toward rung 2, so the answer holds.\"}\nC101\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"A $10 billion investment is financially significant and distinct from the earlier aggregate commitments question. Advanced talks and consideration do not establish completion within days; the reported IPO timetable extends into November, supporting the negative forecast{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"A $10B anchor stake in a ~$2T Anthropic IPO is material to NVIDIA's cash and its strategic exposure. The material shows only talks and consideration, with listing expected before November, so completion by 20 September is unlikely.\"}\nC099\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"A $10 billion investment is material to NVIDIA and distinct from earlier questions. Reports describe consideration and advanced talks, not an agreement; the short deadline and the ‘up to’ amount make No the better-supported choice.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"A $10B anchor stake in Anthropic is material to NVIDIA's cash and exposure, and it opened before the March-2027 twin. All material reports talks or consideration of 'up to' $10B, unconfirmed, with no agreement days before the deadline.\"}\nC023\n{\"worth\": \"trivia\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"A single FOMC meeting decision is a macro event, not an NVIDIA bet. It cannot change NVIDIA's revenue, profit, cash or credit risk. Given the material, the 86% futures pricing plus hawkish July dissents justify forecasting rung 1.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"trivia\", \"answer\": \"insufficient\", \"why\": \"A single routine rate decision is too indirect to be a material NVIDIA-specific bet. Futures probabilities forecast a hike but do not establish the meeting’s actual decision by the deadline.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"trivia\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"Keeping supported: the answer is a forecast, and 86% futures pricing, up from 60%, plus hawkish July dissents and inflation data clearly favour rung 1. That is not too thin to choose an answer.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"trivia\", \"answer\": \"insufficient\", \"why\": \"One rate decision is too indirect to be a material NVIDIA-specific bet. Futures pricing supports a forecast, but the pre-meeting material does not establish the decision made by September 16.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"trivia\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"Keeping supported. The material is a forecast basis, not a record of the outcome, but 86% futures pricing (up from 60%), hawkish July dissents and core PCE above 3% clearly favour rung 1 over rungs 2 and 3.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"trivia\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"A single rate decision is too indirect to be a material NVIDIA-specific bet. The reported 86% futures probability supports a hike as the most likely forecast, though it does not establish the actual decision.\"}\nC055\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"A DOJ challenge to a $17-20B Groq deal could force remedies affecting NVIDIA's inference roadmap, and no earlier question covers it. The investigation has no timetable, and DOJ often closes reviews quietly, so 'still open' fits.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"insufficient\", \"why\": \"The antitrust review concerns a roughly $20 billion transaction and could materially affect NVIDIA. The evidence establishes an active inquiry but supplies no timeline or basis for judging a public resolution less likely than no resolution by January 31.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"The material shows an active inquiry with a recent information request and no sign of imminent action. DOJ conduct reviews usually run long and often close without public statement, so 'still open' beats a public conclusion within 4.5 months.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"insufficient\", \"why\": \"The review concerns a roughly $20 billion transaction. The material establishes an active inquiry but gives no basis for judging whether a public resolution by January is less likely than no qualifying public resolution.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"The material shows an active, intensifying inquiry and a recent formal information demand, with no settlement or complaint signals. DOJ conduct probes typically run long and often close silently, so 'still open' rightly beats a published conclusion within 4.5 months.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"insufficient\", \"why\": \"The roughly $20 billion transaction is material. The inquiry and information request establish no timetable or basis for comparing a public resolution with no qualifying resolution by January; the claimed typical DOJ timelines are not in the supplied material.\"}\nC196\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"insufficient\", \"why\": \"The DOJ review concerns a major NVIDIA transaction and is distinct from earlier questions. An ongoing inquiry without a resolution timetable does not establish whether it is more likely to remain open or conclude by January 31.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"A DOJ challenge, unwind or consent decree on a $20B arrangement is material and distinct from the financing-arrangements question. The material shows an active review with a fresh information request and no timetable, so 'still open' is justified.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"insufficient\", \"why\": \"The review concerns a major transaction and is distinct from earlier questions. An active inquiry without a resolution timetable does not establish whether it is more likely to conclude or remain open by January 31.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"Keep supported. A newly reported formal information request, no timetable, and DOJ's habit of rarely announcing closures make 'still open' the reasoned default. The material is enough to prefer rung 3; nothing points to a conclusion or abandonment.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"insufficient\", \"why\": \"The major Groq arrangement warrants tracking and is distinct from earlier questions. The reported inquiry and information request establish no resolution timetable; the material does not justify preferring continued openness over a public conclusion by January 31.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"Keep supported. The review is active, a formal information request was only recently reported, and no timetable exists. Investigations at that stage rarely resolve within four months, so the material justifies rung 3 over rung 1. Nothing suggests abandonment.\"}\nC110\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"The commitments are large enough to affect NVIDIA’s financial risk. The $36 billion July baseline, typically spanning six years, supports a year-end estimate above $20 billion despite utilization and paused deals; overlap with lease guarantees remains uncertain.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"$36B of six-year commitments under the new model is a material credit-risk bet, first in the list. Pause reports don't extinguish existing commitments, and modest utilization run-down plus the $3.5B lease guarantees still leaves well above $20B.\"}\nC154\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"The model’s potentially multibillion-dollar backstops could materially affect NVIDIA’s financial risk. The $36 billion disclosed commitment and typical six-year term support the top bracket, although capacity consumption makes the pro-rata estimate uncertain.\"}\n{\"worth\": \"worth\", \"answer\": \"supported\", \"why\": \"NVIDIA filed $36B of six-year take-or-pay backstop commitments under the model, a scale that moves cash and credit risk. Plausible run-off or the reported pause cannot bring it below $10B by FY2027 year-end, so rung 5 holds.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
