{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'Anthropic|hyperscalers|AI cloud customers’ guarantees' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-{2,3}/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl --max-columns 220 --max-columns-preview | cut -c1-285; rg -n 'C(023|074|091|099|101|108|110|148|154|196)' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/deck/readings.json | head\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:1:{\"case\": \"new-2/clean-5\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The topic tracks two annual figures\\u2014the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditures for 2026 and for 2027\\u2014wi\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:39:{\"case\": \"new-2/clean-43\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The topic's outcome tracks both completion of NVIDIA's announced investment in Anthropic and the resulting size of NVIDIA's equity stake, wi\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:56:{\"case\": \"new-2/clean-59\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The tracked outcome is the combined annualized revenue run rate of Anthropic, OpenAI and xAI/SpaceX, not NVIDIA's own figure. These other co\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:3:{\"case\": \"new-3/clean-4\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The topic tracks two annual figures\\u2014the four largest AI hyperscalers\\u2019 combined data center capital expenditures for calendar 2026 an\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:31:{\"case\": \"new-3/clean-34\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The topic's outcome names several distinct tracked results\\u2014the investment's final amount, its completion, its terms and NVIDIA's equity\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:32:{\"case\": \"new-3/clean-30\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"ladder\", \"reason\": null, \"requests\": [{\"request_sha256\": \"390c1e2cd6cb759424a42f82aa52908891dc9d01b2f436bcfb40aa661cfa1bec\", \"answer_text\": \"{\\n  \\\"no_ladder\\\": nu\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:33:{\"case\": \"new-3/clean-35\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"ladder\", \"reason\": null, \"requests\": [{\"request_sha256\": \"d28eda4e5dbd09548601775db397c3c836d070aaad9053bb24bab502b78233e8\", \"answer_text\": \"{\\n  \\\"no_ladder\\\": nu\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:57:{\"case\": \"new-3/clean-58\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The outcome tracks both NVIDIA signing and disclosing an anchor investment commitment and Anthropic completing its IPO, without identifying \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib,sqlite3,collections\nroot=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run\\\")\ndef ro(p): return sqlite3.connect(p.absolute().as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nfor n in (\\\"new-2\\\",\\\"new-3\\\"):\n r=root/n\n cases={row[\\\"thesis\\\"][\\\"id\\\"]:row for row in json.loads((r/\\\"cases/cases.json\\\").read_text())}\n con=ro(r/\\\"judge\\\"/f\\\"draw-{n}\\\"/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")\n print(n,\\\"cases\\\",len(cases))\n rows=list(con.execute(\\\"select l.thesis_id,l.view_id,l.status,l.reason,v.question from clean_ladders l left join views v using(view_id)\\\"))\n print(\\\"all:\\\",collections.Counter(x[2] for x in rows),\\\"final:\\\",collections.Counter(cases[x[0]][\\\"thesis\\\"][\\\"opens_as\\\"] for x in rows if x[-1]),\\\"unmatched:\\\",sorted(set(cases)-{x[0] for x in rows}))\n for i,row in enumerate(json.loads((r/\\\"cases/cases.json\\\").read_text())):\n  t=row[\\\"thesis\\\"]; des=t[\\\"description\\\"].lower()\n  if any(k in des for k in (\\\"anthropic\\\",\\\"hyperscal\\\",\\\"hugging face\\\",\\\"venture\\\",\\\"federal reserve\\\",\\\"rates\\\",\\\"fomc\\\",\\\"tariff\\\",\\\"h100\\\",\\\"lpu\\\",\\\"alliance\\\",\\\"groq\\\",\\\"capital expend\\\",\\\"stock\\\")):\n   print(i,t[\\\"id\\\"],t[\\\"opens_as\\\"],t[\\\"description\\\"],[(x[2],x[3]) for x in rows if x[0]==t[\\\"id\\\"]])\n con=ro(r/\\\"step1/trace.sqlite\\\")\n print(\\\"sample result shape:\\\",str(con.execute(\\\"select substr(result,1,1400) from trace where result is not null limit 1\\\").fetchone())[:1000])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,pathlib,sqlite3,collections\nroot=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run\\\")\ndef ro(p): return sqlite3.connect(p.absolute().as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nfor n in (\\\"new-2\\\",\\\"new-3\\\"):\n r=root/n\n cases={row[\\\"thesis\\\"][\\\"id\\\"]:row for row in json.loads((r/\\\"cases/cases.json\\\").read_text())}\n con=ro(r/\\\"judge\\\"/f\\\"draw-{n}\\\"/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")\n print(n,\\\"cases\\\",len(cases))\n rows=list(con.execute(\\\"select l.thesis_id,l.view_id,l.status,l.reason,v.question from clean_ladders l left join views v using(view_id)\\\"))\n print(\\\"all:\\\",collections.Counter(x[2] for x in rows),\\\"final:\\\",collections.Counter(cases[x[0]][\\\"thesis\\\"][\\\"opens_as\\\"] for x in rows if x[-1]),\\\"unmatched:\\\",sorted(set(cases)-{x[0] for x in rows}))\n for i,row in enumerate(json.loads((r/\\\"cases/cases.json\\\").read_text())):\n  t=row[\\\"thesis\\\"]; des=t[\\\"description\\\"].lower()\n  if any(k in des for k in (\\\"anthropic\\\",\\\"hyperscal\\\",\\\"hugging face\\\",\\\"venture\\\",\\\"federal reserve\\\",\\\"rates\\\",\\\"fomc\\\",\\\"tariff\\\",\\\"h100\\\",\\\"lpu\\\",\\\"alliance\\\",\\\"groq\\\",\\\"capital expend\\\",\\\"stock\\\")):\n   print(i,t[\\\"id\\\"],t[\\\"opens_as\\\"],t[\\\"description\\\"],[(x[2],x[3]) for x in rows if x[0]==t[\\\"id\\\"]])\n con=ro(r/\\\"step1/trace.sqlite\\\")\n print(\\\"sample result shape:\\\",str(con.execute(\\\"select substr(result,1,1400) from trace where result is not null limit 1\\\").fetchone())[:1000])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"new-2 cases 60\nall: Counter({'ladder': 33, 'no ladder': 27}) final: Counter({'binding_obligation': 12, 'policy_stage': 9, 'product_node': 7, 'shared_quantity': 5}) unmatched: []\n4 clean-5 shared_quantity What will the big-four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditures be over 2026 and 2027 — the aggregate demand pool underpinning NVIDIA's data-center revenue? [('no ladder', \"The topic tracks two annual figures—the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditures for 2026 and for 2027—without naming either as the main figure. A single judged question and ladder cannot cover both years, and selecting one would arbitrarily discard an equally tracked outcome.\")]\n7 clean-8 shared_quantity What will the prevailing market GPU rental price for NVIDIA's data-center GPUs be in 2026 — e.g., the 6-year fixed and the 1-year (short-tenor) GB300 rental rates — as backstop-backed neocloud capacity grows? [('no ladder', \"The topic's outcome tracks both six-year fixed and short-tenor GPU rental rates in calendar 2026 without naming either as the main figure. These are distinct prices and cannot be combined into one ladder or reduced to one without choosing a priority the topic does not provide.\")]\n15 clean-16 shared_quantity How much AI-accelerator deployment capacity will the named AI labs (Microsoft, Meta, Oracle, OpenAI, Anthropic) commit to AMD's Helios rack-scale system in 2026–2027 — i.e., how much of the accelerator demand pool that NVIDIA's data-center revenue depends on is going to a rival rack system? [('no ladder', 'The tracked Helios deployment capacity covers calendar 2026–2027, not one fiscal or calendar year, and neither the topic nor the item specifies a publication day for that combined figure. The listed disclosures do not establish a dated release that would settle it.')]\n16 clean-17 product_node Will NVIDIA's first Groq-based inference chip, the Groq 3 LPU — sold only as a 256-chip Groq 3 LPX rack packaged alongside a Vera Rubin NVL72 — reach first commercial shipments before the end of calendar 2026? [('ladder', None)]\n23 clean-24 shared_quantity What will the used/resale value of NVIDIA's deployed data-center GPUs (H100-class parts first) be over 2026–2027 — i.e., how fast do NVIDIA accelerators depreciate as loan collateral, versus the long-lived 'infrastructure asset' valuation that the $500 billion GPU-backed financing pipeline depends on? [('no ladder', 'The tracked figure covers calendar 2026–2027 rather than one fiscal or calendar year, and neither the topic nor the item supplies a publication date for the resale benchmarks or collateral valuations. The proposed year-end checks are assessment dates, not publication dates, so they do not satisfy the exception for a nonannual period.')]\n25 clean-26 shared_quantity How much of the 2026–2027 AI-accelerator demand pool at the big hyperscalers and AI labs will go to in-house or custom ASICs — Google's TPUs, Amazon's Trainium, Microsoft's Maia and Broadcom's custom XPU programs — rather than to NVIDIA merchant GPUs, and does that captive-silicon volume materially erode NVIDIA's accelerator share and pricing power? [('no ladder', 'The tracked custom-ASIC accelerator capacity covers the two-calendar-year period 2026–2027, not one fiscal or calendar year, and neither the topic nor the item gives a publication day for that combined figure. Quarterly disclosures through 2027 do not supply a publication date for the two-year total.')]\n26 clean-27 shared_quantity What will the major GPU neoclouds' (CoreWeave, Nebius and comparable operators) combined data-center capex and NVIDIA-GPU purchases be in calendar 2026 and 2027 — the debt-financed slice of the accelerator demand pool that sits outside the big-four hyperscalers? [('no ladder', 'The topic names several tracked figures without selecting one main figure: combined GPU-neocloud data-center capex for calendar 2026 and 2027, and the portion spent on NVIDIA GPUs in each year. Its outcome does not identify a single figure and year to judge.')]\n29 clean-30 policy_stage Will FERC finalize tariff provisions/orders that accommodate large loads (AI data centers) and their co-located generation — the reforms the ISOs/RTOs were directed to respond to by late November — and by when? [('ladder', None)]\n32 clean-33 product_node Will NVIDIA begin commercial shipments of its China-market variant of the Groq-licensed Language Processing Unit (LPU) — an export-compliant inference co-processor adapted in software to run with China-available processors — to Chinese customers by end-2026, re-establishing a China data-center/inference revenue stream? [('ladder', None)]\n38 clean-39 shared_quantity How much global venture capital will be invested in AI companies in calendar 2026 — the funding pool that supports AI-native start-ups' compute purchases and NVIDIA's AI-native/ACIE demand? [('ladder', None)]\n40 clean-41 binding_obligation Will NVIDIA complete a definitive acquisition of Hugging Face — the leading open-source AI model hub — reported at $12.9 billion (talks valuing it above $13 billion), and at what final purchase price and consideration? [('no ladder', \"The topic's outcome names several tracked outcomes—signature of a definitive agreement, completion or collapse of the acquisition, and final purchase price and consideration—without identifying one as primary. A single judged event or figure cannot be selected without changing what the topic tracks.\")]\n41 clean-42 policy_stage Will the Trump administration impose new tariffs on semiconductors in fiscal 2027 — duties that would change NVIDIA's chip costs, pricing or demand — or formally decide not to proceed? [('ladder', None)]\n42 clean-43 binding_obligation Will NVIDIA complete its announced investment of up to US$10 billion in Anthropic, and how large will its equity stake in the AI lab become? [('no ladder', \"The topic's outcome tracks both completion of NVIDIA's announced investment in Anthropic and the resulting size of NVIDIA's equity stake, without identifying either as the main outcome. These are separate event and figure questions and cannot be combined into one ladder.\")]\n43 clean-44 binding_obligation How large is NVIDIA's equity exposure to companies sitting on the same AI demand curve — AI labs (xAI/SpaceX, OpenAI, Anthropic), neoclouds (Nebius, CoreWeave) and silicon/optics suppliers (Intel, Coherent, Synopsys) — and how much of its reported profit and book value depends on their valuations, so that an AI-spending slowdown would hit NVIDIA's chip revenue and the value of its investees in the same quarter? [('no ladder', \"The topic names several tracked outcomes without identifying one as primary: the carrying/fair value of NVIDIA's AI-ecosystem equity investments, unrealized gains embedded in earnings, and the sensitivity of profit and book value to investee de-ratings. These cannot be combined into a ladder for one figure or event.\")]\n48 clean-49 shared_quantity What will the combined AI-server revenue, orders and backlog of the major server OEMs — HPE, Dell and Supermicro — be in their fiscal 2027, i.e. the enterprise/OEM channel through which NVIDIA GPUs reach non-hyperscaler customers? [('no ladder', \"The topic names several distinct quantities—combined AI-server revenue, orders, backlog and implied NVIDIA GPU demand—without identifying one main tracked figure. The reported figures also belong to HPE, Dell and Supermicro, not NVDA; their connection to NVIDIA demand does not make them NVIDIA's own figures.\")]\n50 clean-51 shared_quantity What will the prevailing all-in capital cost of building one gigawatt of AI data-center capacity be in calendar 2026–2027 — the unit build cost that determines how much AI capacity a dollar of neocloud or hyperscaler capex buys and that lenders underwrite as facility collateral? [('no ladder', 'The tracked build-cost figure covers calendar 2026–2027, not one fiscal or calendar year, and neither the topic nor the item gives its publication date. The proposed 2027-06-30 checkpoint is an assessment date, not a publication date for the benchmarks or filings that would settle the figure.')]\n52 clean-53 policy_stage Will the U.S. Department of Justice's antitrust review of Nvidia's ~$20 billion licensing arrangement with Groq conclude with an enforcement action (challenge, restructuring or consent decree) rather than closing without action — and would that force changes to Nvidia's access to Groq's technology and to its ability to keep the licensing structure in place? [('ladder', None)]\n58 clean-59 shared_quantity Will the leading AI labs' (Anthropic, OpenAI and xAI/SpaceX) combined annualized revenue run rate reach at least $180 billion by year-end 2026 — the level Brad Gerstner says is required to keep the AI-infrastructure buildout that underpins NVIDIA's data-center demand intact — versus the roughly $100 billion run rate he cites for July 2026? [('no ladder', \"The tracked outcome is the combined annualized revenue run rate of Anthropic, OpenAI and xAI/SpaceX, not NVIDIA's own figure. These other companies' revenues are not a matter covered by the listed themes; their relevance to AI capital spending and NVIDIA's demand does not make the tracked figure NVIDIA's revenue or aggregate AI capital spending.\")]\nsample result shape: ('{\"new_theses\": [{\"local_id\": \"T1\", \"description\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, and does that contingent exposure become large enough to change its credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported amount of NVIDIA\\'s guarantees / credit-support commitments extended to AI cloud customers under the new model.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA\\'s next annual report (Form 10-K), set by section 3 rule for an obligation framework already undertaken; sized at the next annual report.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA\\'s annual report (Form 10-K) and quarterly filings disclosing guarantees and credit-support commitments, with any confirmed partner-level guarantee amounts.\", \"closest_open_thesis\": null, \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"The open thesis list is empty, so this is the only tracked question on NVIDIA\\'s contingent liabilities from this model; the size of the credit support can grow with the AI\nnew-3 cases 60\nall: Counter({'ladder': 41, 'no ladder': 19}) final: Counter({'binding_obligation': 15, 'policy_stage': 10, 'product_node': 9, 'shared_quantity': 7}) unmatched: []\n3 clean-4 shared_quantity Will the four largest AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures reach roughly $650 billion in calendar 2026, and Nvidia's projected $1 trillion in calendar 2027? [('no ladder', 'The topic tracks two annual figures—the four largest AI hyperscalers’ combined data center capital expenditures for calendar 2026 and calendar 2027—without designating either as the main judged figure. They cannot be combined into one annual figure ladder.')]\n9 clean-10 shared_quantity What will the spot rental price for an hour of Nvidia H100 GPU time be by the end of calendar 2026, as custom accelerators and an easing GPU shortage pressure compute pricing? [('ladder', None)]\n28 clean-29 shared_quantity How many in-house AI accelerators (Google TPU, Amazon Trainium, Microsoft Maia) will the three largest hyperscalers collectively produce or deploy in calendar 2027, and how much of their AI compute does that move outside Nvidia's GPU supply? [('no ladder', 'The outcome names several measures without identifying one as primary: combined chip production or deployment volume, deployed compute capacity, and comparison with Nvidia GPU shipments. Production, deployment and capacity are not interchangeable figures, so selecting one would redefine what the topic tracks.')]\n29 clean-30 shared_quantity How much combined data-centre capital expenditure will the major AI neoclouds — CoreWeave, Nebius and comparable operators — commit to in calendar 2026, the AI-infrastructure demand pool outside the four largest hyperscalers? [('ladder', None)]\n32 clean-33 policy_stage Will FERC finalize tariff provisions or rules to speed transmission-grid integration of large loads and co-located generation for AI data centres by 2027, or will the process remain unresolved? [('ladder', None)]\n33 clean-34 binding_obligation How much will NVIDIA ultimately invest in Anthropic under the reported commitment of up to $10 billion, and what equity stake will it hold, as disclosed in its periodic filings? [('no ladder', \"The topic's outcome names several distinct tracked results—the investment's final amount, its completion, its terms and NVIDIA's equity stake in Anthropic—without identifying one as the main figure or event to judge.\")]\n34 clean-35 shared_quantity How much will the largest AI hyperscalers and related companies (including Nvidia) raise in bond issuance during calendar 2026 — does it reach S&P Global's projected ~$400 billion by year-end, up from ~$225 billion issued through end-July? [('ladder', None)]\n35 clean-36 product_node Will NVIDIA's newly tailored China-market inference chip — a modified Language Processing Unit (LPU) built with licensed Groq technology and designed to be US-export compliant — reach first commercial shipments to Chinese customers by the end of calendar 2026? [('ladder', None)]\n36 clean-37 binding_obligation How much will NVIDIA have committed in total to its licence-and-hire deals — the $6bn non-exclusive licence of Poolside's Model Factory software plus a $1bn equity investment in Poolside at a $12bn pre-money valuation, and the prior Groq ($20bn) and Enfabrica (~$900m) arrangements — and at what final amounts will the Poolside transaction close, as disclosed in its periodic filings? [('ladder', None)]\n39 clean-40 product_node Will NVIDIA's Groq 3 LPX rack — the first commercial product from its $20 billion Groq asset purchase — be online and revenue-generating at neocloud Nebius by the end of calendar 2026? [('ladder', None)]\n42 clean-43 binding_obligation Will NVIDIA complete its reported acquisition of Hugging Face for about $12.9 billion (or a valuation above $13 billion), and on what final terms, including regulatory approval and closing? [('ladder', None)]\n43 clean-44 shared_quantity How much global venture capital funding will flow into AI companies in calendar 2026 — and how much of it (roughly 70%, by NVIDIA's account) is spent on compute — the AI-native demand pool NVIDIA says now sits on its platform? [('ladder', None)]\n46 clean-47 policy_stage Will the CFTC approve CME Group's planned cash-settled futures on the hourly rental cost of Nvidia H100 and B200 GPUs — launched against Silicon Data benchmarks — allowing the contracts to go live, and on what conditions (benchmark standardization and transparency) will approval rest? [('no ladder', \"The tracked outcome is CFTC approval of CME Group's compute-rental futures, a matter of derivatives-market regulation that none of the listed themes covers. It is not an outcome about NVDA's own business merely because the contracts reference Nvidia GPU rental costs.\")]\n51 clean-52 shared_quantity Will the Federal Reserve raise its federal funds target range at the September 15–16, 2026 FOMC meeting, as markets priced at roughly a 60% probability, and thereby lift the debt-financing costs of the AI data-centre buildout? [('ladder', None)]\n53 clean-54 policy_stage Will the US Department of Justice conclude its antitrust review of NVIDIA's $20 billion Groq licensing/acqui-hire arrangement with an enforcement action, negotiated remedies or conditions, or close it without action? [('ladder', None)]\n57 clean-58 binding_obligation Will NVIDIA sign and fund its reported US$10 billion anchor investment in Anthropic's initial public offering, and will Anthropic complete that listing on its targeted pre-November-2026 timetable? [('no ladder', \"The outcome tracks both NVIDIA signing and disclosing an anchor investment commitment and Anthropic completing its IPO, without identifying either event as the main one. A single judged question cannot be selected without rewriting the topic's outcome.\")]\n58 clean-59 shared_quantity What will the combined annualized revenue run rate of the leading AI labs (Anthropic, OpenAI and xAI/SpaceX) be at the end of calendar 2026, and does it reach the roughly $180 billion Brad Gerstner says is needed to keep the AI infrastructure buildout intact? [('no ladder', 'The tracked figure is the AI labs’ combined annualized revenue run rate at end-2026, not revenue covering one fiscal or calendar year. Neither the topic nor the item gives a publication date for that figure; year-end specifies its measurement date, not when the settling disclosures or reports will be published.')]\nsample result shape: ('{\"new_theses\": [{\"local_id\": \"T1\", \"description\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing AI-factory model, as disclosed in its periodic filings?\", \"outcome\": \"The disclosed size of NVIDIA\\'s guarantees, credit support or other contingent backstop obligations to AI cloud partners (or confirmation that none is material).\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA\\'s FY2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027), the first annual report after the 2026-07-01 item; the item gives no size or signing date, so the next annual report within 18 months of the item is the check date.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA\\'s Form 10-K and quarterly Form 10-Q filings, which disclose guarantees, credit support arrangements and other contingent obligations.\", \"closest_open_thesis\": null, \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"No open thesis exists; NVIDIA\\'s contingent credit exposure to AI cloud partners is a question an analyst would bet on\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/DESIGN-1.6.md | sed -n '45,62p'; rg -n 'company.figure|company_figure|own' docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/d400_code_check.py; nl -ba clean-arch/PRD.md | sed -n '370,407p'; rg -n '\\\"case\\\": \\\"new-(2/clean-(5|41|43|53)|3/clean-(4|34|43|54|58))\\\"' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-{2,3}/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl --max-columns 250 --max-columns-preview | cut -c1-312\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^## D(665|666|667|668) '\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/exposure_census.py | sed -n '1,17p;43,63p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/classify_reads.py | sed -n '61,100p'; rg -n 'company_figure\\\": true|\\\"matter\\\": \\\"M(01|21|22|23|29)\\\"' docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/census/matters.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/DESIGN-1.6.md | sed -n '45,62p'; rg -n 'company.figure|company_figure|own' docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/d400_code_check.py; nl -ba clean-arch/PRD.md | sed -n '370,407p'; rg -n '\\\"case\\\": \\\"new-(2/clean-(5|41|43|53)|3/clean-(4|34|43|54|58))\\\"' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-{2,3}/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl --max-columns 250 --max-columns-preview | cut -c1-312\"","aggregated_output":"    45\t## 1. D400, all three passes\n    46\t\n    47\t- **Code parts** (`d400_code_check.py`): Stage A's accounting, line 6, on each new draw's three ladder passes. The ladder folder comes\n    48\t  from `run.json`, and every `outcomes.jsonl` is checked against its fingerprint in `eligibility.json`.\n    49\t  - A skipped ladder component (no question opened) is \"not measured\".\n    50\t  - Stored two-week runs (c1 to c3) could not measure the due-day line (7 to 11 theses with three ladders, 20 needed). The full window\n    51\t    has about 8 times the articles.\n    52\t  - \"Figure ranges meet end to end\" is enforced at the write (`ladder_step.check_answer`).\n    53\t- **Reader lines 1–5:** Stage A's reading, unchanged, run per draw. Line 5's minimums scale as D452 scaled them\n    54\t  (`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/READING-PLAN.md`): ceil(35N/44) right and ceil(28N/44) right by a ladder. D400's 35 of 44 and the\n    55\t  actual N are printed beside.\n    56\t  - `d400_reader_lines.py` recomputes line 5 from `collect_readings.py report`'s counts (right, right by a ladder, of N) with the\n    57\t    scaled minimums. `accounting.py` hard-codes 35 and 28, so its own line 5 is not read. N = 0 reads \"not measured\" (D452). `collect_readings.py` reads one ladder folder and its packet files:\n    58\t  `build_reader_packets.py <key> --run <ladder folder>=<cases.json> --readers <draw's reader folder>`, then\n    59\t  `collect_readings.py rows | disputes | report` on that folder.\n    60\t\n    61\t## 2. Step 2's three lines on draw new-1 (hard)\n    62\t\n2:step's three passes, by Stage A's own accounting (`clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_a/accounting.py`, line 6), with no reader row.\n4:- the favourable end is the same in all three sends of every thesis whose three sends are company figures (measured with at least 3 such);\n6:- figure ranges meet end to end: the answer-choice step's own check refuses a ladder whose ranges do not (`ladder_step.check_answer`), so\n   370\t### Two parts\n   371\t\n   372\t1. **The ladder step (Step 4a).** One call per newly opened thesis, after Step 3 has saved the item that opened it and before that\n   373\t   day's judging. It reads the thesis, the article that opened it, the company and its fiscal-year note, and the list of themes; it\n   374\t   reads nothing later than the opening article. It writes the judged question, its answer choices and its due day, and lists every\n   375\t   other part of the thesis as not judged. Model: gpt-6-luna at medium effort as frozen (D426); astra (gpt-6-astra) is the candidate under test (D648, D649, D653).\n   376\t2. **The judge (Step 4b).** Production's frozen judge (judging-v7, D354), unchanged. Once a day it judges every judged question whose\n   377\t   evidence changed, citing the evidence it used. On a question's due day production's closing step records the final answer (D567).\n   378\t\n   379\t### What the ladder step decides\n   380\t\n   381\t- **One judged question per thesis**, the part the thesis's outcome names. Every other part is listed as not judged and shown beside\n   382\t  the answer (D397). A second side of the same bet is context, not a second question (D436).\n   383\t- **An event** has at most three outcomes: it happened, it is ruled out, or still open by the due day. Code writes \"still open\"\n   384\t  (D409). Ruling out is stated as who decides and what they decide (D416); a proceeding that formally ends without the conclusion\n   385\t  asked for is ruled out (D420).\n   386\t- **A figure** has exactly five ranges set against a reference, with the favourable end registered and its reason; code orders and\n   387\t  labels the ranges (D398, carrying D186). An amount already reported may be the reference, labelled as that and never as the market's\n   388\t  expectation (D411). Where the texts do not settle it, an amount the company owes or guarantees has the lower end favourable (D413).\n   389\t- **The owner**: the company, or a theme. A government's permission or ban on a class of product belongs to the theme (D418).\n   390\t- **The due day**: for an event, a stated date or the last day of the thesis's period (D403); for a year's figure, code sets it to the\n   391\t  fiscal year-end plus 60 days for a company and the calendar year-end plus 90 days for a theme (D414).\n   392\t- **A decline** is allowed with its reason: no main part, a rolling twelve months with no publication day (D417), or no theme that\n   393\t  covers the matter. An answer refused twice by code writes no question (D405). A thesis without a judged question is kept, and the\n   394\t  links to it are counted, not judged.\n   395\t\n   396\t### Where it stands\n   397\t\n   398\t- **Built and frozen as it ran in the fourth Stage A** (D426). A second look by the model at its own answer was tested and not adopted\n   399\t  (D425).\n   400\t- **Gate 1 (Stage A) is not met.** With astra on the frozen request (D649), lines 1, 3 and 6 were met and line 4 missed by 7 (the D436 class); with D414 applied to 12 due-day points, line 5 is met and line 2 hinges on one disputed point (D653). In the fourth run on Alex's 44 theses, lines 2 and 6 were met, line 5 on two of three readings, and\n   401\t  lines 1, 3 and 4 missed by 1, 2 and 3 answers of 131. Every later reading on fresh topics also missed it (nine readings in all).\n   402\t- **Gate 2 (Stage B) is not met.** Three draws, 236 judgements: unsupported figure answers 3.4%, 3.2% and 10.5% against a limit of\n   403\t  12.7–14.4%, and yes/no answers 0.9%, 0.8% and 4.9% against 5% (both met); the control line could not be exercised (4 comparable\n   404\t  controls of 8 needed), and the strictest reading of the last-answer line missed (3 of 24)\n   405\t  ([result](step4-2026-11-10/stage_b/STAGE_B_RESULT.md)). Re-sending the judge cannot close the control shortfall (D576, D592).\n   406\t- **On the product's page** the judged question, its answer choices and the parts not judged are shown without any invented direction\n   407\t  (D570–D573).\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:1:{\"case\": \"new-2/clean-5\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The topic tracks two annual figures\\u2014the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditures for 2026 and for 2027\\u2014without naming either as the \npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:38:{\"case\": \"new-2/clean-41\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The topic's outcome names several tracked outcomes\\u2014signature of a definitive agreement, completion or collapse of the acquisition, and final purchase price and co\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:39:{\"case\": \"new-2/clean-43\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The topic's outcome tracks both completion of NVIDIA's announced investment in Anthropic and the resulting size of NVIDIA's equity stake, without identifying either as\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-2/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:55:{\"case\": \"new-2/clean-53\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"ladder\", \"reason\": null, \"requests\": [{\"request_sha256\": \"298b258c88a8f9f2b9028efa60a3cf2d276c86e4d80fee524774096ef21a6642\", \"answer_text\": \"{\\n  \\\"no_ladder\\\": null,\\n  \\\"ladder\\\": {\\n    \\\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:3:{\"case\": \"new-3/clean-4\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The topic tracks two annual figures\\u2014the four largest AI hyperscalers\\u2019 combined data center capital expenditures for calendar 2026 and calendar 2027\\u2014withou\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:31:{\"case\": \"new-3/clean-34\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The topic's outcome names several distinct tracked results\\u2014the investment's final amount, its completion, its terms and NVIDIA's equity stake in Anthropic\\u2014wi\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:47:{\"case\": \"new-3/clean-43\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"ladder\", \"reason\": null, \"requests\": [{\"request_sha256\": \"f89f9bfe2372e7a93ad0e5408104ec4373f4e604f7e9db8ba86048af52202ce7\", \"answer_text\": \"{\\n  \\\"no_ladder\\\": null,\\n  \\\"ladder\\\": {\\n    \\\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:51:{\"case\": \"new-3/clean-54\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"ladder\", \"reason\": null, \"requests\": [{\"request_sha256\": \"8fa2dcec395b09ff27d6f4381d158e1799bc79a28136db1ad8ff527d3abfaac5\", \"answer_text\": \"{\\n  \\\"no_ladder\\\": null,\\n  \\\"ladder\\\": {\\n    \\\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/ladder/send-1/outcomes.jsonl:57:{\"case\": \"new-3/clean-58\", \"pass\": 1, \"status\": \"no ladder\", \"reason\": \"The outcome tracks both NVIDIA signing and disclosing an anchor investment commitment and Anthropic completing its IPO, without identifying either event as the main on\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^## D(665|666|667|668) '\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/exposure_census.py | sed -n '1,17p;43,63p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/classify_reads.py | sed -n '61,100p'; rg -n 'company_figure\\\": true|\\\"matter\\\": \\\"M(01|21|22|23|29)\\\"' docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/census/matters.json\"","aggregated_output":"9398:## D665 · 2026-11-17 · Council (D664, Alex's \"run your council to determine\"): the six lines stop blocking release; one product comparison against the running pipeline decides readiness, its verdict written before the first send (council consensus)\n9443:## D666 · 2026-11-17 · Alex: write a run plan for the release comparison, monitor it carefully, no scope drift\n9460:## D667 · 2026-11-17 · Under D666 and D439 (council, run-plan rounds 1–10): the release comparison's run plan and the rules it takes for this run\n9519:## D668 · 2026-11-17 · Under D667 (council): the old replays are all reads; the scored window is 08-31 to 09-20; every kept article in it is read for the census\n     1\t\"\"\"Exposure census of the scored window (D667 item 2, D668; plan §1.2): which of the window's articles any past artifact holds, by id or by\n     2\ttext sha256. Every file under the roots is read (`.git` and this comparison's own folder excepted):\n     3\t- SQLite files table by table, row by row (never as raw bytes, so an id split across overflow pages is still found);\n     4\t- tar archives member by member (SQLite members are copied to a scratch file and read the same way);\n     5\t- every other file as bytes.\n     6\t\n     7\tA table holding `input_id` and `content_sha256` is exempt only when its (input_id, content_sha256) pair set equals a build listed in the\n     8\tfrozen `corpus-builds.json`. Its other tables are still read. The corpus-wide search index is exempt by path.\n     9\t\n    10\t    python3 exposure_census.py <output folder> <scratch folder>\n    11\t\n    12\tWrites census.json (population, hits per article, exclusions, kept articles per week, unscanned files, controls). Refuses to write when a\n    13\tcontrol fails.\n    14\t\"\"\"\n    15\timport datetime\n    16\timport gzip\n    17\timport hashlib\n    43\t\n    44\t\n    45\tdef population():\n    46\t    \"\"\"The scored window's articles by the clean selection rule: {input_id: content_sha256} and {id: day}.\"\"\"\n    47\t    conn = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{SOURCE}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    48\t    ids, days = {}, {}\n    49\t    for iid, day, identifiers, text, feed, copy_of, supersedes, sha in conn.execute(\n    50\t            'select input_id, publication_day, object_identifiers, text, feed, copy_of, supersedes, content_sha256 from items '\n    51\t            'where publication_day between ? and ?', WINDOW):\n    52\t        tickers = [x.get('value') for x in json.loads(identifiers or '[]') if x.get('kind') == 'ticker']\n    53\t        if 'NVDA' in tickers and text and feed == 1 and not copy_of and not supersedes:\n    54\t            ids[iid], days[iid] = sha, day\n    55\t    return ids, days\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\tdef roots():\n    59\t    listing = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', str(ROOT), 'worktree', 'list', '--porcelain'], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout\n    60\t    trees = [Path(line.split(' ', 1)[1]) for line in listing.splitlines() if line.startswith('worktree ')]\n    61\t    return trees + [BACKUPS]\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    61\t    _folders[folder] = result\n    62\t    return result\n    63\t\n    64\t\n    65\tdef classify(location):\n    66\t    \"\"\"'storage', 'pool', 'code-only' or 'read'.\"\"\"\n    67\t    inner = location.split('!')[-1]\n    68\t    in_archive = '!' in location\n    69\t    if '::' in inner:\n    70\t        table = inner.rsplit('::', 1)[1]\n    71\t        if table == 'items':\n    72\t            return 'storage'\n    73\t        if table == 'run':\n    74\t            return 'pool'\n    75\t        return 'read'\n    76\t    name = inner.rsplit('/', 1)[-1]\n    77\t    if name in ('manifest.json', 'selection.json'):\n    78\t        return 'pool'\n    79\t    if not in_archive and name.endswith(DATA_FILE) and folder_is_code_only(os.path.dirname(location)):\n    80\t        return 'code-only'\n    81\t    return 'read'\n    82\t\n    83\t\n    84\tdef main(census_path, out_dir):\n    85\t    census = json.loads(Path(census_path).read_text())\n    86\t    conn = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{SOURCE}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    87\t    day = dict(conn.execute('select input_id, publication_day from items where publication_day between ? and ?', WINDOW))\n    88\t    window_ids = set(census['kept_ids']) | set(census['hits'])\n    89\t    window_ids = {a for a in window_ids if a in day}\n    90\t    kept, reads_by_location, not_reads = [], collections.Counter(), collections.Counter()\n    91\t    for article in sorted(window_ids):\n    92\t        classes = [(location, classify(location)) for location in census['hits'].get(article, [])]\n    93\t        if article in READER_SHOWN:\n    94\t            classes.append((f'reader shown without id: {READER_SHOWN[article]}', 'read'))\n    95\t        if not any(c == 'read' for _, c in classes):\n    96\t            kept.append(article)\n    97\t        for location, c in classes:\n    98\t            folder = location.split('::')[0]\n    99\t            (reads_by_location if c == 'read' else not_reads)[(c, folder)] += 1\n   100\t    week = lambda d: str(datetime.date.fromisoformat(d) - datetime.timedelta(days=datetime.date.fromisoformat(d).weekday()))\n25:   \"matter\": \"M01\",\n182:   \"company_figure\": true,\n217:   \"company_figure\": true,\n257:   \"company_figure\": true,\n290:   \"company_figure\": true,\n354:   \"company_figure\": true,\n531:   \"company_figure\": true,\n597:   \"matter\": \"M21\",\n618:   \"matter\": \"M22\",\n639:   \"matter\": \"M23\",\n766:   \"matter\": \"M29\",\n806:   \"company_figure\": true,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Will the (DOJ Antitrust Division publish a formal|US Department of Justice publish a decision)|data centre capital|Hugging Face' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L{3,5}-codex.txt | head -22; rg -n 'reports.*720|four.*760|760 billion' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/deck/C148.txt | head -10; rg -n 'M17|M04' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L{1,2}-{codex,claude}.txt; rg -n 'funds target|venture capital' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/deck/C023.txt | head -6\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Will the (DOJ Antitrust Division publish a formal|US Department of Justice publish a decision)|data centre capital|Hugging Face' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L{3,5}-codex.txt | head -22; rg -n 'reports.*720|four.*760|760 billion' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/deck/C148.txt | head -10; rg -n 'M17|M04' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L{1,2}-{codex,claude}.txt; rg -n 'funds target|venture capital' poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/deck/C023.txt | head -6\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L3-codex.txt:19:{\"matter\":\"M19\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"ids\":[],\"main_outcome\":null,\"note\":\"No question on completion of the Hugging Face acquisition.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L3-codex.txt:26:{\"matter\":\"M26\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"ids\":[],\"main_outcome\":null,\"note\":\"No question on EU review of the Hugging Face acquisition.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L3-codex.txt:27:{\"matter\":\"M27\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"ids\":[],\"main_outcome\":null,\"note\":\"No question on the outstanding Hugging Face acquisition obligation.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L3-codex.txt:28:{\"matter\":\"M28\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"ids\":[],\"main_outcome\":null,\"note\":\"No question on U.S. review of the Hugging Face acquisition.\"}\n12:Reason given: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four companies, which is rung 4. A direct four-hyperscaler estimate of $760 billion and tracking near $745 billion support an implied outcome around $750–$760 billion. Newer estimates of nearly $800 billion refer to the top five, not these four, so the fifth company cannot be counted in full; the four-company outcome remains below the $800 billion rung-5 boundary. The revised company guidance and Microsoft's reported capex growth support a high total, while the cancellation, grid-connection and sustainability concerns are not quantified as changes to the four-company 2026 total.\n37:12. (third_party_view; publisher fool.com; speaker fool.com author; original source The four largest hyperscalers; published 2026-08-23) The four largest hyperscalers plan a combined $760 billion of capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n38:   Quote: The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\n102:Previous answer, given for comparison: 4. Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion.; reason: The market reference is the four-company estimate of $720–745 billion, which is rung 4. The newer $760 billion estimate for the four largest hyperscalers also falls in rung 4, so it lifts the implied total within the rung but does not cross the $800 billion boundary. The revised Alphabet and Amazon guidance, Meta guidance, and Microsoft’s reported capex growth support a high total, but do not establish a four-company total of $800 billion or more. Estimates near $800 billion for the top five cannot be assigned in full to these four; the reported cancellations and grid constraints are downside risks but are not sized to move this total below rung 4.\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L1-claude.txt:4:{\"matter\": \"M04\", \"status\": \"opened\", \"ids\": [\"Q5\"], \"main_outcome\": true, \"note\": \"Q5 asks four largest hyperscalers' 2026 data-center capex; effectively the same total capex figure.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L1-claude.txt:17:{\"matter\": \"M17\", \"status\": \"opened\", \"ids\": [\"Q40\"], \"main_outcome\": true, \"note\": \"Same outcome, but Q40's deadline (2026-09-20) is far shorter than 2027-03-14 and does not ask size.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L2-codex.txt:4:{\"matter\":\"M04\",\"status\":\"narrower\",\"ids\":[\"Q6\"],\"main_outcome\":false,\"note\":\"Covers data-center capital expenditure, not the four companies' total capital expenditure.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L2-codex.txt:17:{\"matter\":\"M17\",\"status\":\"narrower\",\"ids\":[\"Q47\",\"Q48\"],\"main_outcome\":true,\"note\":\"Both ask about agreement to invest $10 billion; Q47 uses an earlier deadline, and neither measures an investment of another size.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L2-claude.txt:4:{\"matter\": \"M04\", \"status\": \"opened\", \"ids\": [\"Q6\"], \"main_outcome\": true, \"note\": \"Q6 asks for the four largest hyperscalers' combined 2026 capex. It is framed as data-center capex, which is essentially the same figure.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L2-claude.txt:17:{\"matter\": \"M17\", \"status\": \"opened\", \"ids\": [\"Q48\", \"Q47\"], \"main_outcome\": true, \"note\": \"Q48 matches the 2027-03-14 deadline. Q47 is a short-window version (by 2026-09-20). Neither asks the investment size.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L1-codex.txt:4:{\"matter\":\"M04\",\"status\":\"narrower\",\"ids\":[\"Q5\"],\"main_outcome\":false,\"note\":\"Q5 asks about data-center capex rather than total capex.\"}\npoc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L1-codex.txt:17:{\"matter\":\"M17\",\"status\":\"opened\",\"ids\":[\"Q40\"],\"main_outcome\":true,\"note\":\"Q40 uses an earlier deadline and asks about completing a $10 billion investment.\"}\n1:Question: Will the FOMC raise its federal funds target range by at least 0.25 percentage points at its September 15–16, 2026 meeting, as established by its September 16 policy statement and target-range announcement?\n5:1. The FOMC announces an increase of at least 0.25 percentage points in its federal funds target range at the September 15–16, 2026 meeting, by September 16, 2026.\n6:2. After September 6, 2026, the FOMC formally decides at its September 15–16 meeting to leave the federal funds target range unchanged, lower it, or raise it by less than 0.25 percentage points. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-06 and stands on 2026-09-16, and only when rung 1 does not.\n9:Latest answer at the cutoff: The FOMC announces an increase of at least 0.25 percentage points in its federal funds target range at the September 15–16, 2026 meeting, by September 16, 2026.\n18:Previous answer, given for comparison: 1. The FOMC announces an increase of at least 0.25 percentage points in its federal funds target range at the September 15–16, 2026 meeting, by September 16, 2026.; reason: The reference is the futures market’s roughly 60% probability of a September hike, which places the market expectation on rung 1. The reported strong jobs data, core PCE running just over 3%, and three officials’ preference for a quarter-point hike at the July meeting reinforce a hike as the most likely September outcome. The evidence does not establish that the FOMC has already decided; this is a forecast, and the likely hike crosses the rung 1 boundary.\n53:- opened 2026-08-27: How much total global venture capital funding will AI companies raise in calendar 2026, in USD, as reported in PitchBook's year-end global AI venture-funding data?\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
